window.AUTHEO_DOCS_INDEX = [{"title":"AUTHEO documentation","path":"index.html","text":"AUTHEO documentation AUTHEO brings together a settlement chain and a design for peer connectivity, distributed compute, a capacity marketplace, developer workflows and agent-assisted operations. This documentation explains each component, how they exchange intent and evidence, and which claims have implementation support. Publication candidate This documentation was reviewed on 9 October 2026. Source-backed does not mean deployment-tested. This site does not certify network availability, platform launch status, security guarantees, or regulatory approval. Start with the platform map Autheo is a layered platform: the Autheo L1 underlies everything, the Layer 0 mesh runs workloads, and DevHub deploys to it (The Autheo stack, Layer 0 mesh). It has three surfaces priced and settled in THEO: (1) DevHub, the Autheo Labs IDE toolkit with full-stack SDKs, a workflow/DAG engine and the agentic OS; (2) the Autheo app ecosystem; and (3) the compute and storage marketplace. The platform map explains how they connect, the developer journeys give a path per role, and the roadmap records what is live, source-backed or planned as of 12 October 2026. Surface Read 1 · Build Build with DevHub, DevHub overview, API and CLI, MCP server 2 · Ship App ecosystem 3 · Run Compute and storage marketplace, Developer usage Settle THEO economy, L1 testnet and mainnet Choose your path New to AUTHEO? Start with the orientation and architecture. Application developer? Read integration preparation, then the source-backed API reference. Node operator? Follow node preparation and network reference. Looking for mesh, DevHub, or marketplace instructions? Read the ecosystem map, then Mesh, DevHub, Marketplace or Freddie. These design guides are not interchangeable with the implemented chain. Maintaining these docs? Use contribution rules and standalone deployment. Understand the whole workflow Start with who does what, follow control, data and accounting flows, then prepare an application brief or provider review. Intended capabilities are explained without presenting planning examples as shipped commands. What is verified here? The public chain repository contains Cosmos SDK, CometBFT, and Ethermint/EVM application code. The official network repository publishes a mainnet configuration. Evidence and limits identify exact revisions. No live transaction, validator operation, or RPC availability test was performed for this documentation update. Use the status matrix whenever a capability claim affects your implementation."},{"title":"Getting started","path":"getting-started/index.html","text":"Getting started New here? Read the platform map first. It shows the three Autheo surfaces (DevHub, app ecosystem, compute/storage marketplace), the THEO economy and the L1 testnet and mainnet, and links each to its section. The developer journeys give a step-by-step path for app developers, agent builders, providers, publishers and chain integrators. 1. Identify your task AUTHEO Chain integration, infrastructure operation, and proposed ecosystem services need different instructions. Begin with architecture boundaries, not an unversioned command copied from a planning document. 2. Choose a verified environment Record the network name, exact chain identifier, trusted configuration revision, endpoint operator, software release, and date checked. The network reference records published configuration; it is not a list of tested public endpoints. Do not infer an Ethereum JSON-RPC chain ID from a Cosmos chain-ID string. Do not transfer funds to establish whether a guessed environment works. 3. Choose your next guide Goal Next step Required input Query or integrate with the chain Integration preparation Operator-approved endpoint and API family Run a full node or validator Node preparation Approved release and network configuration Deploy an app on Autheo DevHub Build with DevHub An account at dev.autheo.com Test THEO payments or contracts L1 testnet and mainnet A wallet configured for testnet chain 785 Understand L0, DevHub, Marketplace, Freddie Ecosystem concepts Start with roles and flows; obtain release evidence before execution Fix a docs problem Troubleshooting Page URL and non-sensitive error details Before you run anything Review scripts and their permissions. Keep seed phrases, private keys, access tokens, and personal data out of terminals shared with others and out of issue reports. Use a sandbox and non-production credentials for integration work. Security boundaries explain the limits of this documentation. Understand the design before selecting a product For application hosting, follow DevHub → Marketplace → Mesh, with connectivity beneath execution and L1 alongside accounting. For delegated operations, add Freddie only after defining authority and spending limits. This reading path is useful even before a supported deployment flow is established."},{"title":"Platform map","path":"platform/index.html","text":"The Autheo platform map Autheo is one layered developer platform. The Autheo L1 blockchain underlies everything: it holds THEO and settles payments. Above it, the compute and storage marketplace sells capacity on the Layer 0 mesh, the peer-to-peer compute fabric. DevHub deploys apps and agents to the L0 mesh, and the app ecosystem distributes what developers build. Every section of this documentation belongs to one layer. The red badges are the go-to-market order from the Autheo \"go-to-market 1.0\" plan: 1 DevHub first, 2 the app ecosystem next, 3 the open compute and storage marketplace third. The full walk through the layers, including what happens to one deployment from git push to settlement, is on The Autheo stack. The layers Layer What it is for Who uses it Start here 2 · App ecosystem Publish and distribute apps, packaged services and agents built on DevHub Publishers and their users App ecosystem 1 · DevHub (Autheo Labs) The complete IDE toolkit: deploy, full-stack SDKs, workflow/DAG engine, agentic OS; deploys to L0 App and agent developers Build with DevHub Layer 0 mesh The compute, data and network fabric every deployment runs on Runs underneath DevHub; node operators run it Layer 0 mesh 3 · Compute / storage marketplace Providers sell L0 capacity, buyers attach it to projects; usage is metered Developers who need capacity; providers Compute and storage marketplace, Developer usage Autheo L1 (testnet, mainnet) Token, settlement and durable evidence under everything Everyone; integrators and validators directly L1 testnet and mainnet, THEO economy How value moves A developer builds in DevHub (box 1) and deploys a project. DevHub deploys it to the Layer 0 mesh, on platform nodes or on capacity bought in the compute/storage marketplace (box 3). What it consumes is metered as developer usage and priced in THEO. Finished work is published to the app ecosystem (box 2), where others deploy or use it, which in turn creates more usage. Payments settle in THEO on the Autheo L1. Providers and the platform are paid from the same settlement. What exists today Short version, as of 2026-10-12 (details on the roadmap and live status pages): Live: dev.autheo.com (DevHub dashboard behind sign-in, public docs and pricing in THEO), the DevHub API host, the Autheo testnet EVM RPC (chain 785) and explorer. Built in source, not publicly proven end to end: the L0 mesh fabrics (iroh QUIC networking, Fluid compute, GuardianDB replication), DevHub workflows, Marketplace placement and allocation boundary, THEO wallet checkout and testnet settlement profile, browser and self-hosted nodes, GPU inference pooling. Planned: a public app-ecosystem storefront, first-party typed Autheo SDKs, provider payouts at scale, mainnet settlement. Every page in this documentation labels claims as live (probed on a stated date), source-backed (code at a pinned commit), or planned. When a page and this map disagree, the more specific page wins. Choose your path I want to ship an app: Build with DevHub → DevHub overview → API and CLI reference. I want to build an agent: Agentic OS in DevHub → AOS stack → Autheo MCP server → Identity and agent trust. I want to sell compute or storage: Compute and storage marketplace → Provider preparation. I want to publish to the ecosystem: App ecosystem → Plan an application. I integrate with the chain: L1 testnet and mainnet → Network reference → Integration preparation. I want every path side by side: Developer journeys."},{"title":"The Autheo stack (L1 → apps)","path":"platform/stack.html","text":"The Autheo stack: from L1 to apps Autheo is a layered platform. The Autheo L1 blockchain underlies everything. On top of it, providers sell capacity through the compute and storage marketplace. That capacity forms the Layer 0 (L0) mesh, the peer-to-peer compute fabric. DevHub deploys applications and agents to the L0 mesh, and the app ecosystem distributes what developers build. THEO prices and settles every layer. This layering is the one DevHub's own README uses (\"The Autheo Developer Cloud\": developer experience → application runtime → distributed compute → compute fabric → data fabric → network fabric → identity and trust → resource marketplace → Autheo Network), source README.md. The red badges are the go-to-market order. The layers, bottom up Layer Job Built from Status (2026-10-12) Read Autheo L1 Ordered state, THEO, settlement, durable evidence Cosmos SDK, CometBFT, Ethermint EVM, IBC Testnet RPC live (chain 785); mainnet config published L1 testnet and mainnet Marketplace (3) Sell and buy L0 capacity; pin placement; settle in THEO DevHub marketplace boundary + Marketplace service Source-backed; public host not reachable Compute and storage marketplace Layer 0 mesh Run workloads on many independent nodes iroh QUIC, Fluid compute, microVM cells, GuardianDB Source-backed; serves dev.autheo.com deployments Layer 0 mesh DevHub (1) Turn code into running deployments on L0 Dashboard, API, CLI, SDKs, workflows, agentic OS Live dashboard (sign-in) Build with DevHub App ecosystem (2) Distribute apps, services and agents Immutable releases, presets, templates Releases source-backed; storefront planned App ecosystem What each layer gives the one above L1 → marketplace: a token (THEO) and a settlement contract whose Settlement events DevHub verifies, so a provider is paid and the platform fee is taken in one on-chain transaction. (THEO economy) Marketplace → L0: a placement policy per order: which approved nodes may run a buyer's workload. L0 never falls back to unapproved nodes. L0 → DevHub: compute (functions, containers, sandboxes, GPU), replicated data, routing and TLS across the mesh, with no single cloud region. DevHub → app ecosystem: immutable, capability-declared releases that others can deploy and pay for. One deployment, end to end What happens when you push code. Each step names the source that implements it. Push. A Git push (GitHub App or Composio) or an upload reaches DevHub's API. (DevHub API) Build. DevHub builds in a fail-closed sandbox: Podman with gVisor (runsc), a pinned builder image and quota-limited volumes; repository bytes are copied in, never bind-mounted. (build_executor.rs) Immutable deployment. The result is a content-addressed deployment with its own URL; production is an alias, so promote and rollback are alias moves. Place. Without a marketplace order, DevHub places on healthy platform nodes. With an order, it snapshots the validated placement policy and only uses its approved node IDs. (compute marketplace) Distribute to the mesh. Sealed runtime artifacts move to the chosen L0 nodes; platform state replicates over the iroh mesh through GuardianDB; nodes coordinate by gossip. (Layer 0 mesh) Serve. The fluid gateway routes each request to a static asset or a warm Fluid instance over a multiplexed tunnel; DNS answers with the node nearest the client. Meter. Active CPU, memory, requests and GPU time accumulate per tenant. (developer usage) Settle. Plans and usage are paid in THEO; marketplace orders settle through the fee-split contract, verified against the L1. (THEO economy) Evidence level Steps 1–7 are implemented in DevHub source at 29abc9f and power dev.autheo.com, but these docs did not run a signed-in deployment. Step 8 is source-backed against the testnet profile; mainnet settlement is planned. See roadmap. Where agents fit Agents are DevHub users like any other: the agentic OS gives them tools (Autheo MCP), identity and mandates (agent trust), and models served from the L0 GPU pool. Their actions deploy to the same mesh and settle through the same L1."},{"title":"Developer journeys","path":"platform/journeys.html","text":"Developer journeys Five ways into Autheo, each mapped to the platform map. Each step says what is live, source-backed or planned so you know where the path currently ends. 1. App developer: build → run → pay Step Do Status Sign up Create an account at dev.autheo.com Live Deploy Import a Git repo; get a preview URL per push; promote to production Live dashboard (DevHub) Automate Use the REST API, CLI or the Vercel-compatible SDK API live; CLI/SDK source-backed Add workflows Define durable workflows; watch runs at /workflows Source-backed (Workflow/DAG) Pay Pick a plan in THEO; pay from your wallet Pricing live; checkout source-backed (usage) 2. Agent builder: agentic OS Step Do Status Connect tools Install the Autheo MCP server in your MCP client Source-backed Give the agent an identity Create a did:key identity and a scoped mandate (agent trust) Source-backed Run models Serve a model from DevHub's GPU inference pool Source-backed Act with receipts Policy check → tool call → signed receipt Planned as one path (AOS) 3. Compute or storage provider Step Do Status Prepare Read provider preparation Guide Join the mesh Run a self-hosted node, or donate a browser node at /run-node Source-backed; /run-node behind sign-in List capacity Publish a compute or storage listing in the Marketplace Gated; public Marketplace host not reachable 2026-10-12 Get paid THEO settlement via the fee-split contract on testnet Source-backed (THEO economy) 4. App publisher Step Do Status Cut a release Create an immutable Marketplace release of a project Source-backed (app ecosystem) Package it Start from a template or game preset Source-backed Publish and earn List in the app ecosystem Planned 5. Chain integrator or validator Step Do Status Connect to testnet Chain 785 at testnet-rpc1.autheo.com Live (L1 networks) Use chain APIs Cosmos and EVM API families Source-backed (API reference) Run a node Follow node preparation with an approved release Guide"},{"title":"Roadmap and status","path":"platform/roadmap.html","text":"Go-to-market roadmap and status This page tracks every box in the platform map against the go-to-market order (1 DevHub, 2 app ecosystem, 3 compute/storage marketplace). It records evidence, not promises: \"planned\" means nobody has shown it working yet, not that it will never ship. Last updated 2026-10-12. Scorecard Box Live (probed) Source-backed Planned / gaps 1 · DevHub IDE toolkit dev.autheo.com public site, docs, pricing; dashboard behind sign-in; API host api.shadw.cloud Deploys, previews, rollback, domains, data, logs, CLI, local node Rebrand remaining shadw identifiers; make api.autheo.dev resolve 1 · Full-stack SDKs REST API Vercel-compatible TS client, agent-trust Python/JS, MCP server First-party typed Autheo SDKs (TS, Python, Rust) under one auth model 1 · Workflow / DAG tool /workflows (sign-in) Durable runs, graph manifest, replay/cancel/wakeup, console Visual DAG editor; agent-authored workflows 1 · Agentic OS — JARVIS stack catalog, Autheo MCP (44 tools), agent trust, GPU inference Joined agent path: plan → policy → MCP → DevHub → receipt Layer 0 mesh Serves dev.autheo.com deployments iroh QUIC network, Fluid compute, microVM cells, GuardianDB replication, leases, browser nodes Public provider onboarding; published node/region map; zkauth wiring 2 · App ecosystem — Immutable releases with capabilities/revocation; game presets; templates Public storefront, publisher payouts, review policy 3 · Compute/storage marketplace — (marketplace.autheo.dev unresolved) Listings link-out, placement policy v1, private HMAC routes, browser and self-hosted nodes Capacity holds, allocation callbacks, usage ingestion, public host Developer usage Pricing in THEO Rate card metering, ledger, THEO wallet checkout Metered marketplace usage streams THEO Display price feed /api/market-reference/theo Fee-split settlement V2 verification Mainnet settlement profile L1 testnet RPC (chain 785) and explorer answered Settlement profile autheo-testnet-v1 Published testnet token/contract addresses, faucet docs L1 mainnet Not probed Published config autheo_2127-1 Verified public RPC/EVM chain ID; settlement on mainnet What would move each box to \"live\" DevHub: a public walkthrough (sign-up → deploy → custom domain) recorded against dev.autheo.com, and the CLI published under the Autheo name. App ecosystem: one published release that a second account deploys and pays for in THEO on testnet. Marketplace: one provider listing, one buyer order, a placement-pinned deployment and a verified Settlement event on testnet, all on public hosts. Mainnet: an approved settlement profile, published contract addresses and an audit reference. When one of these happens, update this page, the live status page and the changelog in the same commit."},{"title":"Build with DevHub","path":"platform/build.html","text":"Build with DevHub (surface 1) DevHub is Autheo Labs' complete developer toolkit and the first go-to-market surface. It is live at dev.autheo.com and its source is autheo-os/L0_devhub_deploy (reviewed at commit 29abc9f). DevHub is the developer surface: it builds your code and deploys it to the Layer 0 mesh, which runs on capacity settled in THEO on the Autheo L1 (see The Autheo stack). The vision groups DevHub into four parts; this page documents each one with its real status. Layer What you get Status (2026-10-12) 1. IDE toolkit Dashboard, Git deploys, previews, rollback, domains, secrets, logs, CLI, local node Live dashboard behind sign-in; CLI and local node source-backed 2. Full-stack SDKs REST API, SDK-compatible client, agent-trust SDKs, MCP REST API live; SDKs source-backed; first-party typed Autheo SDKs planned 3. Workflow / DAG tool Durable multi-step workflows with a run graph, cron, queues Source-backed; console at /workflows behind sign-in 4. Agentic OS Agent runtime stack, MCP tools, identities, delegated authority, receipts Pieces source-backed; end-to-end agent path planned 1. IDE toolkit The DevHub dashboard is the developer's home. Public pages describe it; the dashboard itself redirects to sign-in (probed 2026-10-12). What it covers (from the dashboard route tree under ui/app/ and the public features page): Projects and deployments: import a Git repo, a container image or an upload; every push builds an immutable deployment with its own URL; production is an alias, so promotion and rollback are alias moves. Compute types: serverless functions (\"fluid compute\", scale to zero), containers from a Dockerfile, edge functions, sandboxes, cron jobs and queues. Data: GuardianDB replicated storage, serverless Postgres and Redis, a project drive with WebDAV, and storage snapshots. Networking: custom domains and DNS with automatic TLS, regions, firewall, raw ports for game servers. Operations: live logs, monitoring, observability (service graph, Core Web Vitals), activity and audit, usage and billing. GitOps: platform configuration mirrored to the repository as openedge.yaml; a push rebuilds and redeploys. GitHub access goes through a GitHub App or Composio's v3 API (ui/lib/composio.ts). Command line and local development (source-backed, see DevHub API and CLI reference): @shadw/cli is the npm wrapper for the shadw CLI (deploy, projects, domains, databases, workflows, observability). The package and binary still use the platform's earlier working name. @shadw/node boots a real single hive-cloud node locally with npx, the same server binary fleet nodes run, in mock mode for development. Operator CLIs hivectl and fluidctl exist in the same repository. Naming DevHub was developed under the working names shadw and OpenEdge. Package names, the CLI binary, llms.txt and the API host api.shadw.cloud still use them. They are the same product as Autheo DevHub. The live getting-started page names api.autheo.dev, which did not resolve on 2026-10-12; use the host shown in your dashboard's API reference. 2. Full-stack SDKs \"Full-stack SDKs\" means a developer can drive every layer (deploy, data, workflows, marketplace, chain, agents) from code. Today that is assembled from several pieces: Piece Language What it does Status DevHub REST API HTTP 34 documented endpoints for deploys, projects, env, domains, nodes and more Live host; generated reference @openedge/vercel-sdk TypeScript A fork of @vercel/sdk pointed at the DevHub API with a hive_… platform key; existing @vercel/sdk code repoints with one import change (packages/vercel-sdk) Source-backed Starter projects Bun, Node, Next.js, Hono, Express Deployable examples in examples/ Source-backed Autheo Agent Environment Python, plus a JavaScript verifier Agent identities (did:key), scoped mandates, signed receipts (details) Source-backed Autheo MCP server Python (MCP) Agent-facing tools over DevHub, Marketplace and the chain (reference) Source-backed, verified against fixtures Chain access Any EVM or Cosmos client Standard JSON-RPC (testnet chain 785) and Cosmos SDK APIs (L1 networks) Testnet RPC live Planned: first-party, typed Autheo SDKs (TypeScript, Python, Rust) that wrap DevHub, Marketplace, THEO payments and agent trust under one client and one auth model. Until they ship, use the REST API or the Vercel-compatible client for DevHub, and standard EVM tooling for the chain. 3. Workflow / DAG tool DevHub ships a durable workflow engine: multi-step jobs that persist each step, survive restarts and can be resumed, replayed or cancelled. Each workflow publishes a graph, so the console can draw it as a DAG. Source evidence: the admin API in crates/hive-cloud/src/admin.rs and the console bridge ui/lib/wf-console/hive-world.mjs. Operation Endpoint (admin API) List or define workflows (with graph manifest) GET / POST /v1/workflows Start a run POST /v1/workflows/:id/run List runs, read one run GET /v1/workflows/runs, GET /v1/workflows/runs/:id Cancel, replay, re-enqueue, wake up POST /v1/workflows/runs/:id/{cancel,replay,reenqueue,wakeup} Append an event to a run POST /v1/workflows/runs/:id/events Hooks and summary GET /v1/workflows/hooks, GET /v1/workflows/summary The dashboard at /workflows embeds the upstream @workflow/web console, backed by DevHub's real run data through a @workflow/world adapter: runs, steps, events, hooks and the per-workflow graph. Requests carry the team and a bearer token. Cron schedules and queues are separate DevHub primitives that can start workflows. Not yet: a visual drag-and-drop DAG editor. Workflows are defined in code and visualised, not drawn. Agent-driven workflow authoring is part of the agentic OS plan. 4. Agentic OS The agentic OS is the layer that lets AI agents build, deploy and operate on Autheo safely. It is documented in depth in The agent operating stack (AOS); here is how it plugs into DevHub: Kernel and interface: JARVIS collects the stack (scheduler, skill and model routing, governance) and labels every component integrated, adapter or planned. Tools: the Autheo MCP server exposes DevHub, Marketplace and chain reads to any MCP client (Claude, OpenClaw and others). Write and transaction tools stay gated. Trust: the Autheo Agent Environment gives each agent an identity, a scoped mandate with budget, and a signed receipt for every action. Delegated operations: Freddie is the design for persistent, delegated operational agents. Compute for agents: DevHub's GPU pool and managed llama.cpp inference (inference.rs) let a project serve an OpenAI-compatible model endpoint inside the fleet. What runs end to end An agent that plans, calls Autheo MCP, gets a policy decision and receipt, then deploys through DevHub, is planned. Each piece exists separately; the joined path has not been demonstrated. Next Compute and storage marketplace · DevHub overview · Developer journeys"},{"title":"DevHub (Layer 0)","path":"devhub/index.html","text":"DevHub (Layer 0) Autheo DevHub at dev.autheo.com is Autheo's developer cloud: Git deployments, serverless functions, containers, static sites, databases, cron jobs, workflows, domains, logs and game servers, run on a peer-to-peer compute fabric instead of a single cloud region. Its code is autheo-os/L0_devhub_deploy (commit 29abc9f, 2026-10-12). The node software is called Hive. A few identifiers in the code still carry the earlier working name shadw (the CLI binary, its config and environment variables, and a legacy API hostname); this documentation uses Autheo DevHub and dev.autheo.com throughout. This page covers what is implemented and how to use it. The generated DevHub API and CLI reference lists every endpoint and command. How it is built The repository README describes these layers, top to bottom: Layer What it contains Developer experience CLI, SDKs, Git, dashboard, APIs, templates, docs Application runtime Functions, containers, static apps, services, AI Distributed compute \"Fluid compute\", warm pools, autoscaling, microVMs Compute fabric Cloud, edge, private, community and mobile/IoT nodes Data fabric CRDTs, local state, replication, storage, databases Network fabric Iroh/QUIC peer-to-peer, relays, DNS, anycast, edge routing Identity and trust Node identity, encryption, veritas reputation, attestation Resource marketplace Compute, storage, bandwidth, hosting priced in THEO Autheo network Cosmos SDK, IBC, EVM, CometBFT In code these map to Rust crates such as hive-cloud (the node and its admin REST API), hive-p2p, hive-edge, hive-crsql (CRDT SQLite), fluid-compute, fluid-gateway, and the CLIs shadw-cli, hivectl and fluidctl; the dashboard is the Next.js app in ui/. Design direction vs shipped Post-quantum key agreement, hardware attestation, veritas reputation and on-chain settlement are described in the README as design direction. Marketplace ↔ DevHub integration has four private HMAC routes implemented; capacity holds, allocation status, usage ingestion and callbacks are documented in docs/openapi/README.md but not implemented. What is live today Checked anonymously on 2026-10-12: Surface Result https://dev.autheo.com/ and marketing/docs pages (/docs, /features, /pricing, /status, /blog …) Served (HTTP 200) Dashboard routes (/projects, /deployments, /new, /settings/api-keys, /run-node …) Redirect to /sign-in; an account is required /api, /api/market/theo on dev.autheo.com 404 https://api.shadw.cloud/healthz (legacy API hostname still used by the dashboard's API reference) Returned ok https://dev.autheo.com/llms.txt Served, but still describes the product under the legacy name https://api.autheo.dev (named on the live getting-started page) Did not resolve (DNS) So today you use DevHub through the signed-in dashboard, or through the API/CLI with an API key created in the dashboard. See Status for the wider evidence matrix. Authentication Every API request carries Authorization: Bearer \u003ckey>, which scopes it to your team. Create keys under Settings → API keys in the dashboard, or with POST /v1/apikeys once you hold a key. Dashboard sign-in uses Clerk. Deploy with the CLI The DevHub CLI is built from crates/shadw-cli; its binary is still named shadw (the autheo command name shown in the repository README is not built yet). # configure once (writes ~/.shadw/config.json), or export the variables below shadw auth export SHADW_API_URL=https://api.shadw.cloud # current DevHub API host (legacy name); local node: http://127.0.0.1:8786 export SHADW_TOKEN=\u003capi key> export SHADW_TEAM=\u003cteam slug> # optional shadw deploy # clone → build → deploy a Git repository shadw build # build status and streamed logs shadw deployments # list, promote (rollback), delete shadw projects # settings, env vars, redeploy, domains shadw observability # overview, logs, resources, metrics, regions, nodes shadw api # call any endpoint directly Run shadw \u003ccommand> --help for flags. The full command list is in the reference. Deploy with the API The core flow uses four endpoints from the reference: POST /v1/git/deploy starts a build from a Git repository. GET /v1/builds/{id} polls build status and logs. POST /v1/deployments/{id}/promote makes a deployment live (also used to roll back). POST /v1/projects/{project}/env and POST /v1/projects/{project}/domains configure environment variables and domains. Game servers DevHub ships game server presets (for example Team Fortress 2, Don't Starve Together and GoldenEye: Source) with a guided setup in the dashboard. Each preset pins an upstream container image; see docs/game-server-presets.md in the repository. Run a node Operators can contribute compute by running a Hive node. The dashboard's Run a node page (signed in) walks through it; the general model is in the node operator guide. The repository's RUNBOOK.md and docs/LIVE_INFRA_RUNBOOK.md cover self-hosting. Use DevHub from an AI agent The Autheo MCP server exposes read-only DevHub tools (deployments, builds and logs, nodes, cluster and mesh status) when you set AUTHEO_DEVHUB_URL and a DevHub key. Licence DevHub's LICENSE.md is restrictive (an \"Open BSL\"-style licence, per the repository description); it is not MIT."},{"title":"DevHub API and CLI reference","path":"devhub/api.html","text":"Autheo DevHub API and CLI reference Generated from code — do not edit by hand. Produced by scripts/gen-devhub-reference.mjs from autheo-os/L0_devhub_deploy commit 29abc9f68731 (committed 2026-10-12), files ui/lib/api-catalog.ts and crates/shadw-cli/src/main.rs. The catalog lists 34 endpoints in 8 groups and the DevHub CLI (shadw) has 14 top-level commands. Autheo DevHub is the developer cloud at dev.autheo.com; see DevHub (Layer 0) for authentication and what is live. Base URL The catalog documents https://api.shadw.cloud as the API host, a legacy hostname from before the product was unified as Autheo DevHub at dev.autheo.com (create API keys there); paths are identical wherever the API is served. A self-hosted node serves the same admin API on http://127.0.0.1:8786 by default (the CLI's default). Endpoints Group Method Path Summary Deployments POST /v1/git/deploy Clone, build and deploy a Git repository. Returns a build id you can stream logs from. The branch (vs the project's production branch) decides production vs preview. Deployments GET /v1/builds/{id} Poll a build's status and streamed logs. State is one of building, ready or error. Deployments POST /v1/deployments/{id}/promote Promote (or roll back to) a deployment — instantly re-points the production alias to it. No rebuild. Deployments POST /v1/projects/{project}/redeploy Rebuild a project's latest source with the current project settings. Deployments DELETE /v1/deployments/{id} Delete a single deployment and unregister its functions. Projects GET /v1/projects/{project}/settings Read a project's settings (build config, env, domains, production branch, preview protection). Projects POST /v1/projects/{project}/env Add or update an environment variable. Sensitive values are encrypted at rest (ChaCha20-Poly1305). Projects DELETE /v1/projects/{project}/env/{key} Remove an environment variable from a project. Projects DELETE /v1/projects/{project} Delete a project and all of its deployments. Domains GET /v1/domains List the custom domains for the active team. Domains POST /v1/projects/{project}/domains Attach a custom domain to a project. The domain's root label is aliased to the project's current deployment. Domains GET /v1/domains/{domain} Get a domain with its DNS records, nameservers, SSL certificate and connected projects. Domains PUT /v1/domains/{domain}/nameservers Set the domain's nameservers (when delegating the whole zone to shadw). Domains POST /v1/domains/{domain}/ssl/renew Reissue the free managed (Let's Encrypt-style) certificate for the domain. DNS POST /v1/domains/{domain}/records Create a DNS record. shadw's authoritative DNS server answers queries for the domain from these records. DNS PUT /v1/domains/{domain}/records/{id} Edit an existing DNS record (system-managed records are immutable). DNS DELETE /v1/domains/{domain}/records/{id} Delete a DNS record. DNS POST /v1/domains/{domain}/import Bulk-import records (DNS migration) from a list and/or a pasted BIND zone file. Idempotent — exact duplicates are skipped. DNS GET /v1/domains/{domain}/scan Detect a domain's CURRENT public DNS records (via DNS-over-HTTPS) so they can be migrated into the console with one click. Records are returned, not added. Webhooks GET /v1/webhooks List outbound webhooks for the active team. Webhooks GET /v1/webhooks/events List the event types a webhook can subscribe to. Webhooks POST /v1/git/webhook Inbound GitHub webhook. A push to the production branch deploys to production; any other branch or a pull request builds a preview. Webhooks DELETE /v1/webhooks/{id} Delete an outbound webhook. Teams GET /v1/teams List the teams you belong to. Teams GET /v1/teams/{slug} Get a team's details, members and plan. Teams POST /v1/teams/{slug}/members Invite a member to a team. Teams DELETE /v1/teams/{slug}/members/{email} Remove a member from a team. Tokens & API Keys POST /v1/apikeys Create a long-lived platform API key (hive_…) bound to the active team. The plaintext token is returned exactly once — only its SHA-256 hash is stored. Present it as Authorization: Bearer hive_… to scope requests to the key's team. Tokens & API Keys GET /v1/apikeys List the API keys for the active team. Only the display prefix is returned — the full token is shown once at creation and never stored. Tokens & API Keys DELETE /v1/apikeys/{id} Revoke an API key. Requests presenting the revoked token stop resolving immediately. Tokens & API Keys POST /v1/token Mint a short-lived (8 h) JWT bearer token scoped to a team + role. Requires HIVE_JWT_SECRET to be configured on the node (returns 400 otherwise). When JWT auth is enforced, mutating requests must present a JWT. Observability GET /v1/overview Platform overview: deployments, regions, request counters and WAF status. Observability GET /v1/logs Recent request + control-plane events for the active team. Observability GET /v1/resources Live cluster resource totals (CPU, memory, disk) across the mesh and this node's usage. Deployments Create a deployment POST /v1/git/deploy Clone, build and deploy a Git repository. Returns a build id you can stream logs from. The branch (vs the project's production branch) decides production vs preview. Body: Name Type Required Description repo_url string yes Git repository URL to clone and build. branch string no Branch to deploy (defaults to the repo's default branch). project string no Project name (auto-generated when omitted). root_dir string no Subdirectory to build, for monorepos. target \"production\" | \"preview\" no Force the environment; omit to classify by branch. use_cache boolean no Reuse the dependency build cache (default true). Get a build GET /v1/builds/{id} Poll a build's status and streamed logs. State is one of building, ready or error. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description id string yes The build id returned by Create a deployment. Promote a deployment POST /v1/deployments/{id}/promote Promote (or roll back to) a deployment — instantly re-points the production alias to it. No rebuild. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description id string yes The deployment id to promote. Redeploy a project POST /v1/projects/{project}/redeploy Rebuild a project's latest source with the current project settings. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description project string yes The project name. Body: Name Type Required Description target \"production\" | \"preview\" no Environment for the new deployment. use_cache boolean no Reuse the existing build cache (default true). Delete a deployment DELETE /v1/deployments/{id} Delete a single deployment and unregister its functions. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description id string yes The deployment id to delete. Projects Get project settings GET /v1/projects/{project}/settings Read a project's settings (build config, env, domains, production branch, preview protection). Path parameters: Name Type Required Description project string yes The project name. Set an environment variable POST /v1/projects/{project}/env Add or update an environment variable. Sensitive values are encrypted at rest (ChaCha20-Poly1305). Path parameters: Name Type Required Description project string yes The project name. Body: Name Type Required Description key string yes Variable name. value string yes Value (sealed at rest when sensitive). target \"production\" | \"preview\" | \"all\" no Which environments it applies to. sensitive boolean no Encrypt at rest and mask in the UI. Delete an environment variable DELETE /v1/projects/{project}/env/{key} Remove an environment variable from a project. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description project string yes The project name. key string yes The variable name to delete. Delete a project DELETE /v1/projects/{project} Delete a project and all of its deployments. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description project string yes The project name. Domains List domains GET /v1/domains List the custom domains for the active team. Attach a domain to a project POST /v1/projects/{project}/domains Attach a custom domain to a project. The domain's root label is aliased to the project's current deployment. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description project string yes The project name. Body: Name Type Required Description domain string yes The fully-qualified domain to attach. Get a domain GET /v1/domains/{domain} Get a domain with its DNS records, nameservers, SSL certificate and connected projects. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description domain string yes The domain name. Set nameservers PUT /v1/domains/{domain}/nameservers Set the domain's nameservers (when delegating the whole zone to shadw). Path parameters: Name Type Required Description domain string yes The domain name. Body: Name Type Required Description nameservers string[] yes Nameservers to delegate the zone to. Renew the SSL certificate POST /v1/domains/{domain}/ssl/renew Reissue the free managed (Let's Encrypt-style) certificate for the domain. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description domain string yes The domain name. DNS Create a DNS record POST /v1/domains/{domain}/records Create a DNS record. shadw's authoritative DNS server answers queries for the domain from these records. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description domain string yes The domain name. Body: Name Type Required Description type \"A\" | \"AAAA\" | \"CNAME\" | \"TXT\" | \"MX\" | \"NS\" yes Record type. name string yes Subdomain (e.g. \"www\") or \"@\" for the apex. value string yes Record value (IP, hostname, text…). ttl number no Time-to-live in seconds (default 3600). priority number no Priority (MX records). Update a DNS record PUT /v1/domains/{domain}/records/{id} Edit an existing DNS record (system-managed records are immutable). Path parameters: Name Type Required Description domain string yes The domain name. id string yes The record id. Body: Name Type Required Description type string yes Record type. name string yes Subdomain or @ for the apex. value string yes Record value. ttl number no Time-to-live in seconds. Delete a DNS record DELETE /v1/domains/{domain}/records/{id} Delete a DNS record. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description domain string yes The domain name. id string yes The record id. Import DNS records POST /v1/domains/{domain}/import Bulk-import records (DNS migration) from a list and/or a pasted BIND zone file. Idempotent — exact duplicates are skipped. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description domain string yes The domain name. Body: Name Type Required Description records DnsRecord[] no Structured records to import. zone string no Raw BIND-style zone text to parse and import. Scan existing DNS GET /v1/domains/{domain}/scan Detect a domain's CURRENT public DNS records (via DNS-over-HTTPS) so they can be migrated into the console with one click. Records are returned, not added. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description domain string yes The domain name. Webhooks List webhooks GET /v1/webhooks List outbound webhooks for the active team. List webhook events GET /v1/webhooks/events List the event types a webhook can subscribe to. Inbound Git webhook POST /v1/git/webhook Inbound GitHub webhook. A push to the production branch deploys to production; any other branch or a pull request builds a preview. Delete a webhook DELETE /v1/webhooks/{id} Delete an outbound webhook. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description id string yes The webhook id. Teams List teams GET /v1/teams List the teams you belong to. Get a team GET /v1/teams/{slug} Get a team's details, members and plan. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description slug string yes The team slug. Add a team member POST /v1/teams/{slug}/members Invite a member to a team. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description slug string yes The team slug. Body: Name Type Required Description email string yes The member's email. role \"owner\" | \"member\" no Role to assign (default member). Remove a team member DELETE /v1/teams/{slug}/members/{email} Remove a member from a team. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description slug string yes The team slug. email string yes The member's email. Tokens & API Keys Create an API key POST /v1/apikeys Create a long-lived platform API key (hive_…) bound to the active team. The plaintext token is returned exactly once — only its SHA-256 hash is stored. Present it as Authorization: Bearer hive_… to scope requests to the key's team. Body: Name Type Required Description name string yes A label for the key (e.g. \"ci\"). role \"owner\" | \"member\" no Role bound to the key (default \"member\"). List API keys GET /v1/apikeys List the API keys for the active team. Only the display prefix is returned — the full token is shown once at creation and never stored. Revoke an API key DELETE /v1/apikeys/{id} Revoke an API key. Requests presenting the revoked token stop resolving immediately. Path parameters: Name Type Required Description id string yes The API key id. Mint a token POST /v1/token Mint a short-lived (8 h) JWT bearer token scoped to a team + role. Requires HIVE_JWT_SECRET to be configured on the node (returns 400 otherwise). When JWT auth is enforced, mutating requests must present a JWT. Body: Name Type Required Description sub string no Subject / user id embedded in the token (default \"user\"). tenant string no Team slug the token is scoped to (default \"default\"). role \"owner\" | \"member\" | \"service\" no Role embedded in the token (default \"owner\"). Observability Get the overview GET /v1/overview Platform overview: deployments, regions, request counters and WAF status. Get logs GET /v1/logs Recent request + control-plane events for the active team. Query parameters: Name Type Required Description limit number no Max events to return (default 100). Get cluster resources GET /v1/resources Live cluster resource totals (CPU, memory, disk) across the mesh and this node's usage. DevHub CLI commands The DevHub CLI binary, its config file and environment variables still carry the legacy name shadw in code. Every request sends Authorization: Bearer \u003ckey>. Configure with shadw auth (saves ~/.shadw/config.json) or the environment: SHADW_API_URL, SHADW_TOKEN, SHADW_TEAM. Command Purpose shadw auth Authenticate the CLI with your API key (saves ~/.shadw/config.json). shadw deploy Deploy a Git repository (clone → build → deploy). shadw build Inspect a build (status + streamed logs). shadw deployments Deployments — list, promote (rollback), delete, inspect. shadw projects Projects — settings, env vars, redeploy, domains, delete. shadw domains Domains & DNS — records, migration (scan/import), nameservers, SSL. shadw webhooks Outbound webhooks. shadw teams Teams & members. shadw keys API keys. shadw databases Databases. shadw cron Cron jobs. shadw workflows Workflows. shadw observability Observability — overview, logs, resources, metrics, regions, nodes. shadw api Generic API passthrough for any endpoint (escape hatch)."},{"title":"DevHub and OpenEdge","path":"components/devhub.html","text":"DevHub and OpenEdge: from source to deployment Design / planned. DevHub is the intended developer control plane. OpenEdge GitOps is the proposed synchronization bridge between application source and that control plane. This is not a verified login, installation or deployment tutorial. Responsibility boundary The planned path is GitHub → OpenEdge GitOps → DevHub → L0 Mesh, with Marketplace admission/placement and chain settlement alongside the execution path. The short diagram describes the developer experience; it does not bypass commercial or security checks. Source repository: application code, versioned build inputs and review history. OpenEdge: associate repository/branch with a project and synchronize desired deployment configuration. DevHub: expose projects, builds, environments, resource requirements, deployment control, logs, domains and status. Marketplace: match permitted demand with eligible capacity and agreed terms. Mesh: admit and execute the selected workload. DevHub should expose underlying decisions, not hide failures behind a single green deployment badge. Objects readers should distinguish Object Meaning in the design Project Application identity and its source/configuration relationships Build Attempt to produce an artifact from a particular source revision Environment Runtime configuration and separately controlled secrets/policy Deployment Desired artifact/configuration placed into an execution environment Workload instance A specific execution attempt on admitted capacity Domain User-facing ingress configuration, separate from endpoint identity Intended developer workflow Choose a source revision and reproducible build inputs. Connect a project through the supported GitOps integration once one is released. Specify artifact/runtime, resources, region, exposure, secret references, budget and recovery requirements. Review build output and admission/placement decisions before enabling traffic. Observe execution health and logs separately from settlement state. Update through a new revision; validate rollback and data compatibility rather than assuming a reverse Git change undoes every effect. The planning contains sample deployment manifests and CLI verbs. They are sketches, not stable schemas or installable packages. No registry hostname, CLI package, SDK language support, IDE extension or infrastructure-as-code provider is promised here. Failure states that matter A build failure precedes placement. An admission rejection may be caused by budget, policy or unavailable capacity. An execution failure means a specific attempt failed after admission. A settlement delay is a separate accounting outcome and must not be displayed as if the workload never ran. Before using a released integration Verify owner and version; authentication/scopes; repository permissions; webhook validation; artifact provenance; secret delivery; cancellation; rollback; log retention and support. Use application lifecycle preparation to assemble these requirements. Freddie adds delegated operations, not a replacement for these controls."},{"title":"Autheo MCP server","path":"mcp/index.html","text":"Autheo MCP server autheo-os/autheo-MCP is a read-only Model Context Protocol server that lets an AI client (Claude Desktop, Claude Code, OpenClaw or any MCP stdio host) inspect Autheo DevHub, the Marketplace, the chain and the agent trust layer. The tool list is in the generated MCP tool reference. Prototype, not a live-service guarantee The repository README states that the implementation was verified with local contract fixtures and real MCP stdio sessions, \"not proof of access to a live Autheo installation\". Every tool only calls the endpoints you configure. Nothing is enabled automatically. What it can do From the repository README at commit 6634165 (version 0.3.0a1): Area Read-only capability Marketplace Public listings, listing detail and capacity; with a buyer session: orders, placement policies, settlement status; with a provider session: profile, listings, node bindings, orders, reward accounting DevHub (Layer 0) Nodes, deployments and their resources, deployment-derived project discovery and settings, builds and log tails, overview, cluster, mesh, security and wallet reads Private bridge HMAC-signed L0 advertisement reads, optional private CA and client certificate for the gateway's mTLS Estimates Exact-decimal listing price estimates marked authoritative_quote: false. No reservation, checkout, payment, allocation, purchase or deployment is created Chain CometBFT RPC reads and independently configured Cosmos REST reads THEO display price From Marketplace /api/market/theo or an explicit JSON feed; stale or unavailable values are not used for conversions Agent trust (optional) Five inspection tools and autheo://agent-trust that verify a short-lived signed summary from the Autheo Agent Environment with an operator-pinned key It exposes no private keys, wallet custody, payments, approval overrides, mandate issuance or transactions. Install Needs Python 3 and git. git clone https://github.com/autheo-os/autheo-MCP.git cd autheo-MCP python -m venv .venv . .venv/bin/activate # Windows: .venv\\Scripts\\activate python -m pip install -e '.[dev]' # add ,trust for the agent trust tools: '.[dev,trust]' autheo-mcp # or: python -m autheo_mcp.server The server speaks MCP over stdio, so you normally let your client start it rather than running it yourself. Configure All configuration comes from the process environment. .env.example documents the names but is not loaded automatically. Never put credentials in tool arguments. Variable Meaning / default AUTHEO_DEVHUB_URL Private Hive admin origin; default http://127.0.0.1:8786 AUTHEO_HIVE_JWT / AUTHEO_HIVE_API_KEY DevHub bearer identity; the JWT wins if both are set AUTHEO_HIVE_TEAM DevHub team; default personal; never forwarded to the Marketplace AUTHEO_MARKETPLACE_API_URL Marketplace application origin; unset by default AUTHEO_MARKETPLACE_BEARER_TOKEN A valid Clerk Marketplace session for buyer/provider reads (expires; refresh through the deployment's sign-in flow) AUTHEO_MARKETPLACE_URL Private DevHub bridge origin (not the public Marketplace) AUTHEO_MARKETPLACE_HMAC_KEY_ID / AUTHEO_MARKETPLACE_HMAC_SECRET Private bridge signing credentials AUTHEO_MARKETPLACE_CA_FILE, AUTHEO_MARKETPLACE_CERT_FILE / AUTHEO_MARKETPLACE_KEY_FILE Private gateway CA and client certificate (set cert and key together) AUTHEO_RPC_URL CometBFT RPC; default http://127.0.0.1:26657 AUTHEO_REST_URL Cosmos REST base; default http://127.0.0.1:1317 AUTHEO_PRICE_FEED_URL Optional JSON feed {price_usd, status: \"live\"} AUTHEO_REQUEST_TIMEOUT Seconds per HTTP request; default 30 AUTHEO_NETWORK Display label only; default autheo-mainnet Endpoints without a public URL in this documentation (DevHub admin, private bridge) are operator-provided. See Networks for published chain endpoints and Status for what is verified. Connect a client Use the absolute path of the virtual environment's autheo-mcp executable and pass configuration in env. Claude Desktop Add to claude_desktop_config.json (Settings → Developer → Edit Config): { \"mcpServers\": { \"autheo\": { \"command\": \"/path/to/autheo-MCP/.venv/bin/autheo-mcp\", \"env\": { \"AUTHEO_RPC_URL\": \"https://your-rpc.example\", \"AUTHEO_MARKETPLACE_API_URL\": \"https://your-marketplace.example\" } } } } Claude Code claude mcp add autheo --env AUTHEO_RPC_URL=https://your-rpc.example -- /path/to/autheo-MCP/.venv/bin/autheo-mcp OpenClaw and other stdio hosts Register a stdio MCP server whose command is the same autheo-mcp path and whose environment carries the variables above. In OpenClaw this goes in the MCP servers section of the Gateway configuration. First tools to try autheo_get_integration_guide and autheo_get_server_info: local setup and capability map, no network. autheo_marketplace_list_listings: commercial supply with optional type/region filters. autheo_marketplace_get_listing and autheo_marketplace_estimate_listing: detail and local arithmetic. autheo_devhub_list_deployments, autheo_devhub_get_build_logs, autheo_devhub_status: infrastructure inspection. Resources: autheo://network, autheo://marketplace, autheo://capabilities, autheo://integrations (and autheo://agent-trust with the trust extra). Verify your install python -m pytest tests -q python review/smoke_upgrade.py # calls all tools against localhost fixtures over real stdio Licensing The MCP package is MIT. Upstream repositories it was built against keep their own terms; DevHub's LICENSE.md is restrictive and is not MIT."},{"title":"MCP tool reference","path":"mcp/tools.html","text":"MCP tool reference Generated from code — do not edit by hand. This page is produced by scripts/gen-mcp-reference.mjs from autheo-os/autheo-MCP commit 663416577abb (package version 0.3.0a1, committed 2026-10-12) using FastMCP tools/list and resources/list introspection of autheo_mcp.server. The registered surface at that commit is 44 tools and 5 resources. 44 of 44 tools declare the MCP readOnlyHint annotation. A tool being registered does not mean a live Autheo service is reachable: every tool calls only the endpoints you configure (see MCP server). Source-backed, not live-tested The MCP README states the implementation was verified with local contract fixtures and MCP stdio sessions, \"not proof of access to a live Autheo installation\". Descriptions below are the docstrings registered in code. Summary Group Tools Server and setup 2 Chain reads (CometBFT / Cosmos REST) 6 THEO display price 3 Marketplace 11 DevHub (Hive admin API) 11 Legacy DevHub node inspection 6 Agent trust inspection 5 Server and setup Tool Summary autheo_get_integration_guide Local integration map and prerequisites; does not claim a live backend connection. autheo_get_server_info Return information about the Autheo MCP server. autheo_get_integration_guide Local integration map and prerequisites; does not claim a live backend connection. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_get_server_info Return information about the Autheo MCP server. This is a local MCP capability check and does not query the blockchain. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. Chain reads (CometBFT / Cosmos REST) Tool Summary autheo_get_account Retrieve basic information about an Autheo account. autheo_get_balance Retrieve the native THEO balance for an Autheo address. autheo_get_block Retrieve an Autheo block by height. autheo_get_latest_block Get the latest Autheo blockchain block. autheo_get_network_status Get the current status of the Autheo network. autheo_get_transaction Retrieve an Autheo transaction by transaction hash. autheo_get_account Retrieve basic information about an Autheo account. Argument Type Required Default address string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_get_balance Retrieve the native THEO balance for an Autheo address. Argument Type Required Default address string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_get_block Retrieve an Autheo block by height. Argument Type Required Default height integer yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_get_latest_block Get the latest Autheo blockchain block. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_get_network_status Get the current status of the Autheo network. Queries the configured CometBFT RPC endpoint and DevHub health endpoint. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_get_transaction Retrieve an Autheo transaction by transaction hash. Argument Type Required Default tx_hash string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. THEO display price Tool Summary autheo_convert_theo_to_usd Convert a THEO amount into its USD reference value using the Autheo oracle. autheo_convert_usd_to_theo Convert a USD reference amount into THEO using the Autheo oracle. autheo_get_theo_price Return display-only THEO/USD data with freshness status; not settlement authority. autheo_convert_theo_to_usd Convert a THEO amount into its USD reference value using the Autheo oracle. This does not execute a trade. Argument Type Required Default amount_theo number yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_convert_usd_to_theo Convert a USD reference amount into THEO using the Autheo oracle. This is an informational conversion and does not execute a trade. Argument Type Required Default amount_usd number yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_get_theo_price Return display-only THEO/USD data with freshness status; not settlement authority. This tool is intentionally separated from marketplace pricing. Marketplace prices should be denominated in THEO while this oracle provides the external USD reference value. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. Marketplace Tool Summary autheo_get_order Retrieve an Autheo marketplace order. autheo_marketplace_categories Return the major Autheo marketplace resource categories. autheo_marketplace_estimate_listing Local exact-decimal estimate from current listing price; NOT an authoritative checkout quote/reservation. autheo_marketplace_get_capacity Read public capacity projection; does not expose the private mesh advertisement API. autheo_marketplace_get_listing Get live listing detail, constraints and canonical decimal-string price. autheo_marketplace_get_placement_policy Read an order's placement constraints; never schedule or mutate the policy. autheo_marketplace_get_settlement_status Read recorded settlement status. Does NOT verify payments or trigger allocation. autheo_marketplace_list_l0_deployments Private HMAC/mTLS service read of short-lived L0 advertisements. Not public listing inventory. autheo_marketplace_list_listings Browse purchasable Marketplace listings; preserves exact THEO strings and upstream page metadata. autheo_marketplace_list_orders List orders for the configured Clerk session's buyer tenant (upstream limit 100). autheo_marketplace_provider_view Read own provider profile, listings, node_bindings, orders, or infrastructure_rewards; Clerk role/tenant enforced upstream. autheo_get_order Retrieve an Autheo marketplace order. Reads the authenticated Marketplace order. A marketplace order is not a DevHub deployment. Argument Type Required Default order_id string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_marketplace_categories Return the major Autheo marketplace resource categories. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_marketplace_estimate_listing Local exact-decimal estimate from current listing price; NOT an authoritative checkout quote/reservation. Argument Type Required Default listing_id string yes — quantity integer no 1 duration integer no 1 duration_unit string no \"hour\" Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_marketplace_get_capacity Read public capacity projection; does not expose the private mesh advertisement API. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_marketplace_get_listing Get live listing detail, constraints and canonical decimal-string price. Argument Type Required Default listing_id string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_marketplace_get_placement_policy Read an order's placement constraints; never schedule or mutate the policy. Argument Type Required Default order_id string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_marketplace_get_settlement_status Read recorded settlement status. Does NOT verify payments or trigger allocation. Argument Type Required Default order_id string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_marketplace_list_l0_deployments Private HMAC/mTLS service read of short-lived L0 advertisements. Not public listing inventory. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_marketplace_list_listings Browse purchasable Marketplace listings; preserves exact THEO strings and upstream page metadata. Argument Type Required Default resource_type string or null no null region string or null no null limit integer no 25 Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_marketplace_list_orders List orders for the configured Clerk session's buyer tenant (upstream limit 100). Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_marketplace_provider_view Read own provider profile, listings, node_bindings, orders, or infrastructure_rewards; Clerk role/tenant enforced upstream. Argument Type Required Default view string no \"profile\" Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. DevHub (Hive admin API) Tool Summary autheo_devhub_get_build Read DevHub build state and evidence from the real /v1/builds/{id} route. autheo_devhub_get_build_logs Read the last 1–2000 build log lines; never executes a build. autheo_devhub_get_deployment Find a deployment in the team-scoped /deployments response. autheo_devhub_get_deployment_resources Read actual deployment resources from DevHub. autheo_devhub_get_job Retrieve an Autheo DevHub workload/job. autheo_devhub_get_project Retrieve an Autheo DevHub project. autheo_devhub_list_deployments Read deployments visible to the configured DevHub team. autheo_devhub_list_nodes Read and normalize DevHub nodes; hardware totals do not imply available marketplace capacity. autheo_devhub_list_projects List project names derived from visible deployments; undeployed projects are not included. autheo_devhub_search Search deployment-backed DevHub projects and deployments (not a complete project inventory). autheo_devhub_status Read overview, cluster, mesh, security, or wallet metadata; process liveness is not mesh health. autheo_devhub_get_build Read DevHub build state and evidence from the real /v1/builds/{id} route. Argument Type Required Default build_id string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_devhub_get_build_logs Read the last 1–2000 build log lines; never executes a build. Argument Type Required Default build_id string yes — tail integer no 200 Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_devhub_get_deployment Find a deployment in the team-scoped /deployments response. Argument Type Required Default deployment_id string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_devhub_get_deployment_resources Read actual deployment resources from DevHub. Argument Type Required Default deployment_id string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_devhub_get_job Retrieve an Autheo DevHub workload/job. Argument Type Required Default job_id string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_devhub_get_project Retrieve an Autheo DevHub project. Argument Type Required Default project_id string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_devhub_list_deployments Read deployments visible to the configured DevHub team. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_devhub_list_nodes Read and normalize DevHub nodes; hardware totals do not imply available marketplace capacity. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_devhub_list_projects List project names derived from visible deployments; undeployed projects are not included. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_devhub_search Search deployment-backed DevHub projects and deployments (not a complete project inventory). Argument Type Required Default query string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_devhub_status Read overview, cluster, mesh, security, or wallet metadata; process liveness is not mesh health. Argument Type Required Default view string no \"overview\" Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. Legacy DevHub node inspection Tool Summary autheo_compute_quote Generate a simulated compute quote. autheo_compute_search Search reported DevHub hardware, NOT reservable marketplace listings. Use autheo_marketplace_list_listings for purchasable inventory. autheo_get_provider Retrieve information about an Autheo marketplace provider. autheo_get_provider_reputation Retrieve the reputation information for an Autheo provider. autheo_storage_quote Generate a simulated storage quote. autheo_storage_search Search reported DevHub storage hardware, NOT reservable marketplace listings. autheo_compute_quote Generate a simulated compute quote. This function MUST NOT create an order or move THEO. Argument Type Required Default cpu integer yes — ram_gb integer yes — duration_hours number yes — storage_gb integer no 0 gpu string or null no null gpu_count integer no 0 Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_compute_search Search reported DevHub hardware, NOT reservable marketplace listings. Use autheo_marketplace_list_listings for purchasable inventory. Prices are expressed in THEO. USD conversion should be performed separately through the Autheo oracle. Argument Type Required Default cpu integer yes — ram_gb integer yes — storage_gb integer no 0 gpu string or null no null gpu_count integer no 0 max_price_theo_hour number or null no null min_reputation number or null no null Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_get_provider Retrieve information about an Autheo marketplace provider. Providers are represented by DevHub nodes in the L0 deployment. Argument Type Required Default provider_id string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_get_provider_reputation Retrieve the reputation information for an Autheo provider. Currently sourced from DevHub node capacity metadata. Argument Type Required Default provider_id string yes — Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_storage_quote Generate a simulated storage quote. This function MUST NOT create a storage contract or transfer THEO. Argument Type Required Default storage_gb integer yes — duration_days integer yes — replication_factor integer no 1 Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_storage_search Search reported DevHub storage hardware, NOT reservable marketplace listings. Argument Type Required Default storage_gb integer yes — duration_days integer yes — replication_factor integer no 1 max_price_theo_month number or null no null min_reputation number or null no null Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. Agent trust inspection Tool Summary autheo_agent_get_mandate Inspect scoped mandate and DEMO budget summary; no real wallet funds or approvals. autheo_agent_get_passport Read the configured agent's issuer-asserted identity, not a proof of legal ownership. autheo_agent_list_receipts Read up to 20 signed-snapshot receipt summaries; not an independent action/chain proof. autheo_agent_trust_guide Explain agent trust setup and boundaries. Does not issue identity, authority, or wallet access. autheo_agent_trust_status Inspect a pinned-issuer signed snapshot and audit summary. Never grants execution authority. autheo_agent_get_mandate Inspect scoped mandate and DEMO budget summary; no real wallet funds or approvals. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_agent_get_passport Read the configured agent's issuer-asserted identity, not a proof of legal ownership. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_agent_list_receipts Read up to 20 signed-snapshot receipt summaries; not an independent action/chain proof. Argument Type Required Default limit integer no 20 Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_agent_trust_guide Explain agent trust setup and boundaries. Does not issue identity, authority, or wallet access. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. autheo_agent_trust_status Inspect a pinned-issuer signed snapshot and audit summary. Never grants execution authority. Arguments: none. Annotations: readOnlyHint: true, destructiveHint: false, idempotentHint: true. Resources URI Description autheo://agent-trust Local documentation of the external agent environment's trust boundary. autheo://capabilities Describe the capabilities exposed by this MCP server. autheo://integrations Pinned integration contracts and setup boundaries. autheo://marketplace Description of the Autheo marketplace. autheo://network Basic Autheo network information. Regenerate node scripts/gen-mcp-reference.mjs --source ../autheo-MCP # clean checkout at the commit to document node scripts/gen-mcp-reference.mjs --check # CI: Markdown matches data/mcp-tools.json npm run docs:build"},{"title":"Agent operating stack (AOS)","path":"concepts/aos-stack.html","text":"The Autheo agent operating stack (AOS) The agent operating stack is the set of pieces an AI agent needs to do real work on Autheo: an interface, a kernel that schedules and routes work, agent runtimes, governance and trust, tools reached through MCP, models, and infrastructure. JARVIS is the hub where these pieces are collected, tested and wired together. Source: JARVIS src/aos-stack.js (the single catalog the JARVIS landing page renders) at commit a7edfd2 (2026-10-12), and the architecture narrative autheo-os/autheo-stack. Status words Every component carries exactly one status, and JARVIS tests enforce the honesty rules: integrated: code inside JARVIS that tests exercise. adapter: JARVIS code that talks to a separately running system; off by default. planned: not connected yet. At this commit: 14 integrated, 5 adapters, 20 planned. The seven layers Layer Purpose Integrated Adapter (off by default) Planned Interface Where people and systems reach JARVIS JARVIS web workspace, kernel HTTP API OpenClaw channels and Control UI Kernel & routing One scheduler, one execution owner, skill/model routing, context Kernel scheduler + supervisor, skill router, baseline model router, JCP context manifests OpenClaw memory, Mem0, Letta Agents & runtimes Agent loops, workflow policy, agent-company runtimes JARVIS bounded-work policy Paperclip, DADA design skill, last30days research skill OpenClaw agent runtime, Freddie, gm, agentplug Governance & trust Who may do what, under which budget, with which receipt JARVIS governance, extracted OpenClaw policy matcher Autheo Agent Environment Tools & MCP Capabilities reached through MCP MCP TypeScript SDK client, owned fixture MCP server gm-mcp Autheo MCP, Unified CAD MCP, MCP filesystem, GitHub MCP Models Inference agentic-router, LiteLLM, hosted model APIs, Ollama Infrastructure & deploy Ledger, CI, containers, hosting Task event ledger, GitHub Actions CI, static container OpenTelemetry, Langfuse, Autheo DevHub (listed in the JARVIS catalog under both its earlier name Shadw and Autheo DevHub) How the Autheo pieces fit The autheo-stack narrative describes the platform side: Layer 0 / DevHub hosts workloads (DevHub). Marketplace provider orchestration supplies DevHub hosting capacity priced in THEO. A distinct, proposed Layer 1 handles verification and settlement on the Autheo chain (Cosmos SDK / CometBFT with EVM). Autheo MCP gives agents read-only, agent-facing tools over DevHub, Marketplace and the chain. The Autheo Agent Environment gives agents identities, delegated authority and signed receipts (Identity and agent trust). An agent task in this model flows: request → JARVIS kernel → policy check (Autheo Agent Environment, signed receipt) → tool call (MCP) → DevHub or Marketplace → result with receipt. What actually runs today In JARVIS today the tested paths are the live digest task and an owned MCP tool call. The full LLM-driven task path, Autheo MCP inside JARVIS, and DevHub as a JARVIS deploy target are planned. The Autheo Agent Environment adapter signs receipts locally, but the kernel does not yet call it before acting. Add a component JARVIS pins external components by exact commit in upstreams/lock.json, checks a structural contract snapshot, and keeps adapters off by default behind an environment flag. npm run upstream:check reports when an upstream moved; npm run upstream -- update \u003cid> re-pins after the contract diff passes. See JARVIS docs/UPSTREAMS.md."},{"title":"Identity and agent trust","path":"concepts/agent-trust.html","text":"Identity and agent trust The Autheo Agent Environment is the reference trust layer for AI agents: who an agent is, what it is allowed to do, and a tamper-evident record of what it was allowed to do. It gives every agent, person and environment a decentralized identifier (DID), lets authority be delegated in narrowing chains, and records signed, hash-linked receipts that anyone can verify offline. Source: SolutionsAsService/autheo-agent-environment version 0.3.0, commit 442b9e5 (2026-10-12), MIT licence. An older 0.1 copy is at autheo-os/autheo-agent-environment. Local reference prototype This is a tested local simulation, not a production identity authority, wallet, sandbox, payment processor or agent-migration platform. Budgets are DEMO units, and no action is executed, even when policy says allow. Not available yet: on-chain anchoring, guaranteed revocation freshness (keep validity short), live-tested did:web, and encrypted key storage (demo DID keys are plain local files). Concepts Concept What it is DID A did:key identifier (Ed25519) created offline, with no registry or server. Agents, principals and environments each have one. Passport A signed statement binding an agent id, its owner and its public key (v1 model). Mandate / delegation Authority to act: environments, scopes, resources, tools, per-action and total budgets, an escalation threshold, validity and maximum depth. In v2 it is a W3C Verifiable Credential 2.0 (AutheoDelegation) signed with Data Integrity eddsa-jcs-2022. Delegation chain Up to 10 links from a root DID the verifier pins (for example human → agent → sub-agent). Each link can only narrow authority; widening is refused when issuing and rejected when verifying. Invocation An agent-signed request to perform one action under a chain. Decision The policy engine's result: allow (reserves simulated budget), escalate (needs human review; there is no agent-callable override) or block (consumes nothing). Every check is explained. Receipt ledger Per-environment SQLite ledger of signed, hash-chained receipts; tampering is detected by ledger verify. Revocation A signed record from an issuer, ancestor or subject that anyone can relay; verifiers reject chains containing revoked links. Checkpoint A signed export that hands a task's state to another environment. It carries no funds, private keys, environment variables or execution authority. Try it Python 3.11+. git clone https://github.com/SolutionsAsService/autheo-agent-environment.git cd autheo-agent-environment python -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate python -m pip install -e '.[dev]' python -m autheo_agent_environment.demo --output demo-output # no network calls python -m autheo_agent_environment.webapp # browser walkthrough on http://127.0.0.1:8765/ The demo creates ephemeral keys, issues a passport and a 100-DEMO-unit mandate, shows allow → escalate → block, writes signed receipts, exports a checkpoint to a second environment, and exports a 120-second read-only summary for the Autheo MCP server. Delegate authority between DIDs (v2) D=\"python -m autheo_agent_environment did\" $D create --out human.json # private key stays in the file (mode 0600) $D create --out agent.json $D create --out sub.json $D delegate --key human.json --to did:key:\u003cagent> --environment 'env:*' --scope compute.simulate \\ --resource 'listing:*' --per-action 80 --total 100 --threshold 50 --depth 1 --out chain1.json $D delegate --key agent.json --to did:key:\u003csub> --parent chain1.json --environment env:research \\ --scope compute.simulate --resource listing:demo-01 --per-action 30 --total 50 --threshold 20 \\ --out chain2.json # can only narrow $D invoke --key sub.json --chain chain2.json --environment env:research \\ --action compute.simulate --resource listing:demo-01 --amount 20 --out inv.json $D verify chain inv.json --root did:key:\u003chuman> # offline: no state, no network $D revoke --key agent.json --chain chain2.json --out rev.json && $D record-revocation rev.json To record a decision in a local environment and get an offline-verifiable bundle: $D trust --environment env:research --root did:key:\u003chuman> $D evaluate --invocation inv.json --bundle-out bundle.json python -m autheo_agent_environment.verifier bundle bundle.json --root did:key:\u003chuman> Bundles also verify in a browser or Node with the dependency-free JavaScript verifier interop/js/autheo-did.mjs. Run it as a local trust service Agent runtimes (for example JARVIS and Paperclip) can call a persistent local service: A=\"python -m autheo_agent_environment --state-dir .autheo-state\" $A init # dev issuer key, API token, policy.yaml, ledgers $A identity issue --agent-id agent:jarvis-1 --owner-id human:operator \\ --environment env:research --public-key-file agent-public.pem $A mandate grant --agent-id agent:jarvis-1 --environment env:research \\ --scope tool.invoke --resource repo:jarvis --tool git.status --per-action 50 --total 100 --threshold 40 $A evaluate --request-token-file request.jwt # decision + explanation + receipt $A ledger verify --environment env:research # exit 1 on tampering $A serve # loopback API on 127.0.0.1:8780 (bearer token) Policies are declarative (autheo.policy/v1, YAML or JSON): scope/tool/resource allow and deny globs, windowed and lifetime budgets, rate limits, time windows and escalation rules. The repository's docs/API-AND-CLI.md, docs/openapi.json (OpenAPI 3.1), docs/POLICY.md, docs/SPEC-DID.md, docs/SPEC-SIGNATURES.md and docs/THREAT-MODEL.md are the detailed references. How it connects to the rest of Autheo Autheo MCP: five optional read-only tools and autheo://agent-trust verify the environment's short-lived signed summary with an operator-pinned key. MCP never issues mandates or approves escalations. JARVIS: an off-by-default adapter turns JARVIS governance outcomes into signed v1 receipts (JARVIS_AUTHEO_ENABLED=1). DevHub (Layer 0): docs/DEV-PORTAL-LAYER0.md defines the planned integration boundary; nothing in DevHub calls the trust service yet."},{"title":"Freddie agents","path":"components/freddie.html","text":"Freddie: delegated agent operations Design / planned. Freddie is the proposed persistent agent harness for transactions, operations and upkeep. Its purpose is to continue a multi-step job over time, not merely answer a prompt. No supported AUTHEO Freddie release or permission contract was verified here. Intended role An agent runs inside or alongside a workload, has an addressable identity, receives events and coordinates work. Planning examples include renewing a capacity lease, restarting a degraded service, coordinating build or AI tasks, collecting results and reporting progress. A typical design flow is trigger → coordinator → scoped worker tasks → result/evidence collection → requester update. The harness must retain task identity and completion state across retries or disconnections. A persistent process alone does not establish durable task execution. Relationships to other components DevHub presents deployment intent and operational status. Marketplace supplies offers, admission and agreement-related workflows. Mesh carries communication and executes agent/worker processes. L1 handles supported chain transactions; an agent's report is not chain finality. Freddie consumes those interfaces. It must not silently redefine payment rules or treat peer connectivity as authority to act. Authorization is a separate design gate The planning explicitly leaves autonomy and spending policy unresolved. Before unattended use, a release needs: Boundary Questions that must be answered Principal Who delegated this task, and for which project/environment? Allowed actions Which reads, deployments, purchases and administrative actions are permitted? Money and signing What caps, asset/network restrictions and confirmation requirements apply? Delegation Can a worker delegate again, and do restrictions follow every subtask? Revocation How quickly does withdrawal of authority stop queued and active work? Audit Which request, approval, tool action and outcome can be reconstructed? Recovery Which operations are safe to retry, and how are duplicate side effects prevented? These are requirements, not claims of implemented controls. Do not provide a wallet seed or unrestricted signing key to an unverified harness. Example: recover a service without exceeding authority A monitoring event can propose inspecting health and logs. A restart may be allowed within the existing deployment. Moving to a different provider, changing data locality or increasing budget may require fresh approval. The agent should report proposed, authorized, running, failed and completed distinctly, and verify the final result rather than equating a submitted tool call with success. Repository indexing, hot reload and code-analysis research are possible development aids, not proof of Freddie runtime features. See developer tooling, security boundaries and capability status. Harness responsibilities behind an agent The tooling research separates the reasoning/control loop, context handling, model-provider adapters, callable tools, procedural skills, conversation history, machine observations, scheduling and observability. This is a useful design decomposition: a model response, a tool execution and a durable task record are different objects. An interface such as a CLI, browser or message channel is an entry point, not necessarily a mandatory serial hop through every other interface. A procedural skill describes how to do work; it does not itself grant permission. A machine snapshot is an observation at a time, not proof that state remains unchanged. Context compression must not silently discard the caller's limits or unfinished obligations. Repository-graph integration is a research possibility, not a verified AUTHEO runtime dependency."},{"title":"App ecosystem","path":"platform/app-ecosystem.html","text":"The Autheo app ecosystem (surface 2) The app ecosystem is where finished work is distributed: apps, packaged services, templates and agents built on DevHub, which other developers and users can deploy or use, paying in THEO. It is the second go-to-market surface. The vision diagram marks it \"marketplace?\" because its exact shape is still a product decision; this page separates what exists from what is proposed. What exists today (source-backed) Releases: the unit of distribution DevHub already has the primitive an app store needs: an immutable Marketplace release of a project (marketplace_releases.rs, docs/marketplace-workload-contract.md). POST /v1/projects/{project}/marketplace-releases creates a release with a platform-issued release_id, revision, source and build identity, publication and revocation state, and declared capabilities. A release is not a deployment: one release can be deployed many times; marketplace allocations bind to the release identity. POST /v1/projects/{project}/marketplace-workloads attaches a paid allocation to a published, unrevoked release at an exact revision. Capabilities are explicit and not inherited. For example, a release that needs a platform-issued client certificate must declare workload_client_certificate: { mode: files-v1 }; rolling back to a release without it fails closed. Packaged apps Game-server presets: a catalog of one-click dedicated servers (for example Palworld, Valheim, Arma 3, Team Fortress 2, Don't Starve Together) with a guided setup: basics, world, access (passwords stored as secrets), advanced options (ui/lib/game-server-presets.ts). These are ordinary image deployments today; paid marketplace game hosting is gated (see compute marketplace). Starter templates: Bun, Node, Next.js, Hono, Express and a Minecraft server in examples/. Integrations: /integrations in the dashboard (sign-in) is the current entry point for connected services; GitHub connection is live through a GitHub App or Composio. Agents and tools Agents and MCP tools are distributable artifacts too. The AOS stack catalog and the Autheo MCP tool reference are the current registries. Proposed listing types A public ecosystem storefront is planned. The listing types below follow from the release model above; they are a proposal for discussion, not a published catalog. Listing type Built from Buyer gets Publisher earns App template A DevHub release A new project deployed from the release One-off or per-deploy THEO fee Packaged service (game server, database, CI runner) A release plus a placement policy A running service on approved capacity Share of the hosting order Agent An AOS agent definition plus mandate template An agent with a scoped mandate and receipts Per-task or subscription THEO MCP tool / integration An MCP server release A tool connected to their agents Usage-based THEO Open questions the storefront must answer before launch: revenue split and how it settles through the fee-split contract; review and revocation policy for published releases; how a buyer verifies the release they get is the one reviewed (release identity plus build provenance); support boundary between publisher and provider. Status Piece Status (2026-10-12) Immutable releases with capabilities and revocation Source-backed Game presets and starter templates Source-backed; deployable as ordinary projects Paid packaged hosting through the marketplace Gated: needs verified provider, binding and advertisement Public app storefront, publisher payouts Planned Next Plan an application · THEO economy · Developer journeys"},{"title":"Plan an application","path":"guides/application-lifecycle.html","text":"Plan an application deployment Audience: application developers evaluating the ecosystem design. Outcome: a reviewable deployment brief, not a live deployment. A released DevHub/mesh workflow is still required. 1. Describe the application Record source revision, build inputs, artifact format, runtime, exposed services and whether the workload is a long-running service, batch job, function or agent. For AI/GPU work, record device/runtime and model requirements rather than assuming generic GPU compatibility. 2. State placement and trust constraints Specify region/locality, data sensitivity, approved provider types, isolation requirements and network exposure. State which constraints are mandatory. A lower price must not silently override them. 3. Separate compute from persistent data List ephemeral state, durable data, backups and restore prerequisites. Define acceptable loss and recovery behavior. Determine whether a retry can repeat side effects or whether a checkpoint/operation identifier is required. 4. Define commercial acceptance Record units, budget cap, expected duration, cancellation/timeout behavior, completion evidence and who may approve spending. Keep these as requirements; do not copy illustrative prices from planning as a quote. 5. Define observation and rollback Track build, admission, execution health and settlement separately. Identify where logs are retained and who can read them. A rollback needs artifact and configuration compatibility plus a data migration strategy; source reversion alone is insufficient. 6. Establish release evidence before connecting Obtain the supported product version, authenticated interface, manifest schema, installation provenance, tested sample and support route. Start with a non-production workload and non-sensitive data. No executable mesh deploy command is supplied because this review did not verify one. Review checklist The brief is complete when another reviewer can identify what runs, where it may run, what it may access, what it may spend, how success is recognized, and what happens on failure. Use DevHub, Mesh, Marketplace and data flows to assign ownership."},{"title":"Layer 0 mesh","path":"platform/l0-mesh.html","text":"Layer 0 mesh Layer 0 (L0) is the compute fabric between the Autheo L1 and DevHub: a peer-to-peer mesh of independent nodes that runs every DevHub deployment. DevHub is the developer surface; L0 is where the work actually executes. Source: autheo-os/L0_devhub_deploy at 29abc9f (the same repository holds DevHub and the mesh node). For the original design discussion (admission, lifecycle, provider trust), see L0 Mesh design and Connectivity. This page documents what the code implements. Status Source-backed and running behind dev.autheo.com. These docs did not operate a node or measure the fleet; public node counts and regions are not published here. The five fabrics Fabric What it does Implemented by Network Encrypted peer-to-peer QUIC with cryptographic node identity, NAT hole-punching and relay fallback; nodes reachable by ID without public IPs hive-p2p (iroh endpoint, relay + DNS discovery), hive-wg (ephemeral WireGuard-style tunnels), gossip.rs (roster and leases over authenticated iroh, HTTP fallback) Distributed compute \"Serverless servers\": per-function pools of long-lived instances, in-function concurrency, reuse before provision, scale to zero fluid-compute, fluid-tunnel (one multiplexed tunnel per instance), hived (control plane, microVM cells, warm pools; Firecracker on KVM) Compute (nodes) Where capacity comes from: platform nodes, self-hosted nodes (Enterprise), GPU nodes, and browser tabs donated via /run-node hive-cloud node binary, gpu_pool.rs, run-node-targets.ts Data Platform state replicated across the mesh; local-first CRDT tables; project storage and databases guardian.rs (GuardianDB: iroh-docs + iroh-blobs, BLAKE3, Willow reconciliation), hive-crsql (cr-sqlite CRR tables, also in browser nodes) Identity and trust Node identity from iroh keys; peer trust gate on connections; single-owner leases for stateful containers hive-p2p, lease.rs (rendezvous hashing + fenced epochs, no global Raft); hive-zkauth (anonymous membership proofs) is an experiment, not wired in Request path The public router, fluid-gateway, picks the deployment by Host, resolves the route to a static asset or a function, and either serves the file or leases a Fluid instance and proxies over that instance's tunnel. DNS answers by client proximity using EDNS Client Subnet (dns_geo.rs), so users reach a nearby healthy node. Stateless vs stateful Functions and static sites are stateless: any node holding the deployment can serve; no coordination needed. Stateful containers (databases, game servers) get exactly one owner at a time. Nodes agree on the preferred owner by rendezvous hashing; a fenced lease epoch stops a partitioned old owner from split-brain; on owner death the next holder takes over with a higher epoch. How L0 connects to the layers around it Up to DevHub: DevHub's API and builds decide what runs; L0 decides where among eligible nodes and runs it. (The Autheo stack) Sideways to the marketplace: a marketplace order's placement policy restricts which L0 nodes may run a workload. The Marketplace service never joins the mesh or sees node keys. (Compute and storage marketplace) Down to the L1: L0 does not run consensus. Payment and settlement facts go to the Autheo L1; logs, artifacts and bulk data stay off-chain. (L1 testnet and mainnet) Run a node Locally, for development: npx @shadw/node boots one real hive-cloud node in mock mode (package still uses the working name). (Build with DevHub) As a provider: self-hosted nodes are an Enterprise feature today; read provider preparation. Public provider onboarding is planned. From a browser: sign in to dev.autheo.com and open /run-node. L0 nodes are not L1 validators. To run a chain node, see node preparation."},{"title":"Compute and storage marketplace","path":"platform/compute-marketplace.html","text":"Compute and storage marketplace (surface 3) The marketplace sits between the Autheo L1 and the Layer 0 mesh: it is where L0 capacity is bought and sold, and where those orders settle on the L1: buyers (developers) attach compute or storage to a DevHub project, and providers (node operators) supply it and get paid in THEO. It is the third go-to-market surface. This page documents the integration that exists in DevHub source at commit 29abc9f (autheo-os/L0_devhub_deploy). The commercial Marketplace application itself is a separate service; for its intended design (offers, matching, disputes) see Marketplace design. Status at a glance (2026-10-12) Capability Status Listings by resource type (compute, storage) linked from a DevHub project Source-backed Placement policy v1: an order pins which nodes may run the workload Source-backed Private DevHub ↔ Marketplace service routes (HMAC-signed) Source-backed THEO settlement on Autheo testnet (MarketplaceAtomicFeeSplit V2) Source-backed, enabled only by an explicit profile Public Marketplace host marketplace.autheo.dev did not resolve on 2026-10-12; /marketplace on dev.autheo.com redirects to /integrations (sign-in) Capacity holds, allocation status callbacks, usage ingestion Planned (deliberately not implemented yet) What can be bought DevHub models two resource types (ui/lib/marketplace.ts): compute: vCPU, memory, disk and execution slots on approved provider nodes; GPU capacity comes from DevHub's GPU pool, grouped by region (gpu_pool.rs). storage: capacity for project data. On the platform side, tenant storage operations go through one storage broker that checks ownership, identifier safety, then capacity, in that order (storage_broker.rs). A third listing type, game_hosting, is accepted by the Marketplace but needs a verified provider, an active binding and a healthy DevHub advertisement before it can be quoted. Demo cards are not purchasable capacity (docs/marketplace-game-hosting-integration.md). Buyer flow: attach capacity to a project In a DevHub project, choose Marketplace (or New Project → Deploy from Marketplace). DevHub sends you to {marketplace}/listings?project=…&resource_type=compute|storage&return_url=…. You sign in to the Marketplace (its own account flow) and place an order. The Marketplace returns you to the project with marketplace_order=\u003cid>; DevHub attaches the order to the project. When you deploy with that order, DevHub fetches the order's placement policy, validates it, and snapshots it into the deployment. Only the approved nodes may run it. Source: docs/marketplace-project-resources.md, docs/devhub-marketplace-placement-policy.md. Placement policy v1 A policy names the order, the buyer tenant, status and revocation state, a validity window, approved_node_ids, provider, listing, region, resources (vcpu, memory_mb, disk_gb) and commercial terms. DevHub rejects it if any of these hold: unknown contract or policy version; tenant or order mismatch with your signed-in session; not active, revoked, or outside its validity window; malformed fields, or fields that look like addresses, credentials or control-plane data. Approved node IDs are a hard allowlist. If none of them is healthy, reachable and has capacity, the deployment fails with MARKETPLACE_PLACEMENT_UNAVAILABLE. DevHub never falls back to an unapproved node. Provider flow: supply capacity There are two ways capacity joins the mesh today: Self-hosted nodes: run the hive-cloud node on your own machines (bare metal, Firecracker microVMs, Macs). Enterprise plans include self-hosted nodes and data residency (pricing). See node preparation for chain validators and provider preparation for compute providers. Browser nodes (/run-node): a signed-in user can donate a browser tab as a mesh node. It holds a relay identity, can serve browser-eligible functions under strict limits, or replicate a project database (ui/lib/run-node-targets.ts). Requests served by browser nodes are counted but not billed today. Marketplace never becomes a mesh peer: it gets no node keys, relay credentials, tenant workloads or private topology (docs/marketplace-l0-routing.md). How the two services talk Direction Route Auth DevHub → Marketplace GET /v1/marketplace/orders/{id}/placement-policy Marketplace session token for the signed-in buyer Marketplace → DevHub GET /v1/marketplace/l0/deployments Timestamped HMAC with nonce replay protection Marketplace → DevHub POST /v1/marketplace/payment-intents same Marketplace → DevHub POST /v1/marketplace/payments/verify same Marketplace → DevHub POST /v1/marketplace/l0/allocations same Marketplace → DevHub POST /v1/marketplace/workloads, /workload-intents/v2 same Allocations carry settlement and resource facts only, never repository URLs, images or build settings. Workloads attach to an immutable, published release of a project (see app ecosystem). Settlement Orders are paid in THEO through the MarketplaceAtomicFeeSplit V2 contract: one transaction splits the gross amount between the provider and the platform fee recipient (fee 500 bps = 5%), and emits a Settlement event that DevHub verifies after 2 confirmations. Details: THEO economy. Known gaps From DevHub's own design notes: the four private routes do not provide capacity holds, allocation status, callbacks or usage ingestion. Fleet-wide capacity reservation is intentionally unimplemented until a store with conditional writes exists, to avoid selling the last unit twice (marketplace_reservations.rs). Do not treat a successful allocation as a capacity guarantee. Next Developer usage and billing · THEO economy · Marketplace design"},{"title":"Developer usage and billing","path":"platform/developer-usage.html","text":"Developer usage and billing \"Developer usage\" is the arrow between the marketplace and the app ecosystem in the platform map: what your projects consume, how it is measured, and how it becomes a THEO charge. Source: crates/hive-cloud/src/billing.rs and the live pricing page (both checked 2026-10-12). Plans The public pricing page denominates plans in THEO and says USD values are display-only. Plan Price Highlights (pricing page) Enforced quotas (source) Hobby 0 THEO, forever 1 concurrent build, preview deploys, HTTPS, 100 GB bandwidth 250 projects, 3 members, 3 running sandboxes, 1 GiB drive, no overage Pro 20 THEO / month Unlimited concurrent builds, domains, roles, build cache, cron and workflows 1,000 projects, 25 members, 10 running sandboxes, 25 GiB drive, pay-as-you-go overage Enterprise 1,000 THEO / month 1,000 THEO of included compute, self-hosted nodes, data residency and leases, SSO, audit logs, SLA Unlimited projects and members, overage What is metered The rate card is the single source of truth for metered pricing; the dashboard reads it and the meter loop charges with it. Dimension Unit Rate card value Notes Active CPU CPU-hour (non-idle only) 12.8 Idle time is free (\"Active-CPU pricing\") Memory GB-hour provisioned 1.06 Edge requests per million 200 WAF-blocked requests per million 60 GPU GPU-hour, wall time 60 T4 class; a GPU is held for the instance's whole life Browser-node requests per million 0 Counted separately, deliberately unbilled today Units Rate-card values are hundredths of the billing unit. Code comments still express them in USD cents, while plans are sold in THEO and THEO checkout converts catalog amounts as hundredths of THEO. Treat the dashboard's usage page as authoritative for what you are charged. Usage is accumulated per tenant from live metrics, charged as deltas, and every balance change goes to an append-only ledger and the audit log. Paying in THEO DevHub's THEO checkout (ui/lib/theo-wallet.ts, theo_network and verify_theo_settlement in admin.rs): You connect MetaMask, Coinbase Wallet or Keplr (EVM mode). The provider object never leaves the page. DevHub shows the operator-configured network: chain ID, RPC, explorer, THEO token address, treasury address, decimals and required confirmations. If any value is missing or unsafe, checkout is disabled instead of guessing. You send an ERC-20 transfer of the exact THEO amount to the treasury. DevHub verifies the transaction on chain: right chain ID, right token contract, recipient is the treasury, amount matches exactly, and the required confirmations have passed. DevHub never holds a wallet secret and cannot sign a payment for you. The THEO/USD price shown beside amounts comes from a display-only feed and is never used to compute what you owe. From usage to providers When a project runs on marketplace capacity, the order is settled separately through the marketplace's fee-split contract, so the provider is paid directly (THEO economy). Per-request usage ingestion from providers into marketplace settlement is planned; today an order is a fixed allocation, not a metered stream. Next THEO economy · App ecosystem"},{"title":"Marketplace","path":"components/marketplace.html","text":"Marketplace: offers, matching and accountable outcomes Design / planned. The Marketplace coordinates demand and provider supply. This page explains its intended commercial workflow; it is not an offer, price list, payment instruction, custody determination or released contract specification. Participants and offerings A buyer states workload/service needs and acceptance terms. A provider advertises resources or a packaged service and accepts execution obligations. The coordination layer discovers offers, checks eligibility, matches demand, correlates evidence and prepares settlement-related actions. The planning distinguishes: Raw compute: CPU, memory, GPU, storage and network capacity with units and eligibility constraints. Packaged services: an application plus hosting configuration, such as a game server, database or CI runner. Developer services: application delivery or ongoing support, possibly bundled with infrastructure. Human-service acceptance needs different evidence from machine resource metering. These are intended categories, not currently purchasable listings. A packaged application still needs maintenance, data protection and an explicit service boundary. Matching is more than finding the lowest price The proposed sequence is validate request → filter eligible supply → rank candidates → reserve/admit → execute → collect evidence → reconcile settlement. Candidate criteria include hardware/runtime fit, region and locality, availability, path quality, provider history and commercial terms. A capacity advertisement may be stale. Matching must be followed by an admission/reservation mechanism with expiry and conflict handling. A private-node requirement must not silently become public placement just because capacity is scarce. Agreement and evidence Information Why it must be explicit Resource unit and time basis Prevent ambiguous usage and price comparisons Completion/acceptance condition Distinguish a successful start from delivered work Usage attribution Correlate provider records with the accepted dispatch Cancellation, timeout and dispute rules Define who may act and what evidence is required Retry identity Avoid duplicate execution and duplicate settlement Recovery/data ownership Establish what survives provider failure The design proposes signed or attributable usage/completion records. A provider's signed assertion attributes a claim; it does not independently prove that the claim is correct. Reputation, attestation and dispute evidence serve different purposes. Settlement design and unresolved mechanics The more consolidated planning prefers buyer-authorized, direct buyer/provider settlement using chain-enforced terms, rather than an operator-maintained customer balance. Older exchange drafts also explore escrow and other market models. Those descriptions are not merged into a single implemented payment protocol. In particular, “funds stay in a wallet,” “contract locks a commitment” and “automatic refund” do not define the actual contract state or authority. A release must specify asset location, signing/approval rights, spend restrictions, timeout execution, fees, acceptance evidence, dispute paths and failure recovery. No deployed contract or verified bridge flow was established here; do not transfer funds based on this design. Reputation and service tiers The proposal uses completion history, availability, latency outcomes and policy/attestation signals to inform provider selection. On-chain anchoring may record durable evidence or summaries; it does not require every raw metric to be stored on-chain. A score is not a guarantee against failure. Best-effort, standard, premium and private capacity are design categories. Availability targets, compensation, reserve capacity and enforcement must be defined before any category becomes an SLA. Historical price examples and projected savings are not current quotes or measured economics. Failure behavior to agree before work starts Provider loss should preserve evidence and distinguish retryable work from stateful recovery. Control-plane loss should not invent a new bill or assume every in-flight workload stopped. Reconciliation needs dispatch identity, usage records and verified chain outcomes. A retry is not permission to charge again. Continue with data flows, provider preparation and status."},{"title":"L0 Mesh","path":"components/mesh.html","text":"L0 Mesh: execution and node lifecycle Design / planned. This explains the proposed execution fabric. No AUTHEO mesh release, installation command, admission service or workload benchmark was verified in this review. What the mesh adds The connectivity layer supplies peer identity, discovery and transport. L0 Mesh adds resource advertisements, node admission, workload placement/execution, health reporting and distributed operational state. Its peer is a machine running the proposed node software, with identity, advertised capacity, local policy and one or more workload runtimes. Providers may be data-center, edge, home, cloud-hosted or GPU operators. These are design categories, not evidence that every host can join. An eligible runtime, network path, security policy and supported hardware profile would all be required. Control and data responsibilities Node control functions Node data functions Advertise available resources and supported runtime classes Fetch artifacts and receive workload inputs Apply admission, quotas and placement policy Execute the workload and carry service traffic Manage warm pools and drain decisions Return results and stream permitted logs Report health and synchronize operational state Retain or transfer state according to recovery policy Marketplace matching identifies commercially eligible supply; the node still needs a local admission decision. An advertisement is not a reservation, and stale capacity must not authorize over-allocation. Workload lifecycle Describe: supply runtime/artifact, CPU/memory/storage needs, network exposure, region, budget and recovery policy. Match and admit: select eligible providers and verify current capacity and local policy before execution. Prepare: obtain artifacts, configure secrets and network access, and select the supported isolation profile. Execute and observe: track start state, health, resource use, logs and completion independently. Finish or recover: return results; preserve evidence; clean up runtime and credentials; retry only when workload semantics permit. Long-running services need health and renewal policies. A batch task needs an unambiguous completion condition. A stateful service additionally needs a data durability and recovery contract; rescheduling a process does not restore its data. Runtime choices and Fluid Compute The principal compute design proposes Firecracker microVMs on suitable Linux/KVM hosts. Other research explores containers, gVisor, Litebox and hardware-assisted confidential execution. These alternatives are not interchangeable guarantees and are not all declared supported by this site. The released runtime profile must settle compatibility, host prerequisites, tenant boundaries and device access. Fluid Compute means keeping eligible runtimes warm so repeated invocations may avoid some initialization. A warm-pool hit is an optimization, not a latency guarantee. Reuse requires a defined tenant boundary, secret cleanup and state-reset policy; exhaustion needs a documented cold-start or admission response. Distributed state and failure behavior The design proposes CRDT-based synchronization for operational state such as availability and workload status. That does not make stale advertisements safe or turn distributed telemetry into authoritative financial state. Reservations, duplicate work, revocation and settlement each need explicit conflict and freshness rules. Failure Required behavior to specify and test Provider disappears Distinguish never-started, failed and unknown outcome; retain evidence before retry Partition Limit new admission when global state is uncertain; reconcile on rejoin Warm capacity exhausted Expose cold-start or capacity rejection, not an unexplained timeout Runtime or policy drifts Stop new placements; drain safely and verify before readmission Stateful workload moves Restore from a tested checkpoint/replica; do not imply automatic data recovery Provider state model Bootstrap → qualify/admit → execute → drain → retire or recover. A draining node stops accepting new work while preserving in-flight obligations. Track admission failures, warm-pool hit rate, launch latency, completion rate, resource saturation, connectivity and policy freshness. No numeric target is established here. Continue with connectivity, provider preparation, and security. Private and hybrid capacity Enterprise planning includes owned hardware, approved provider domains and optional overflow to external capacity. Treat these as distinct placement policies. A private workload must remain within its approved boundary unless the owner explicitly allows overflow; availability pressure is not permission to move sensitive data. Offering spare capacity to other buyers additionally requires tenancy, maintenance and commercial obligations to be defined. None of these patterns is a verified enterprise service in this review."},{"title":"Connectivity","path":"components/connectivity.html","text":"Connectivity: identity, discovery and transport Design / planned. The proposed connectivity substrate lets a workload or node address a peer without treating a fixed IP address as its identity. This page does not assert a deployed AUTHEO transport stack. Four separate questions Question Design responsibility Who is this peer? Key-derived endpoint identity and an explicit key lifecycle Where might it be reached? Discovery records and reachability hints Can a session be established? Direct connection attempts, NAT traversal and relay fallback May it perform this action? Application authorization and node/workload policy Discovery is not authorization. A signed address record can attribute an advertisement without proving capacity, reputation, permission or safe execution. Proposed connection flow Load endpoint identity and local policy. Publish or resolve current reachability hints. Attempt eligible direct paths; use NAT traversal where supported. Establish an authenticated transport session and apply application authorization. Observe path quality and repair or replace degraded paths. Use eligible relays when a direct path cannot be established; keep this visible in diagnostics and capacity planning. The planning discusses Iroh/QUIC transport, Pkarr/DHT address publication and local discovery. Bluetooth, multipath, relay optimization and post-quantum negotiation appear in research/design material; they are not a released capability list. Exact protocol versions and fallback behavior need implementation evidence. Underlay, overlay and edge ingress The design's peer sessions still depend on physical links, IP routing, internet providers and often relay infrastructure. Local communication is an intended option; the whole ecosystem is not independent of the internet. DNS, BGP, anycast, PoPs and submarine cables describe different parts of that underlying environment, not AUTHEO-owned infrastructure. Edge ingress can bring a conventional client request into a service; peer transport connects participating endpoints. Neither decides price or settles a workload agreement. Path locality also does not establish data residency or compliance: artifact placement, backups, logs and relay paths all need separate review. Diagnose by stage Cannot resolve: inspect record availability, freshness and identity binding. Cannot connect: inspect local firewall/NAT policy and allowed relay paths without exposing private topology. Connected but denied: inspect authorization, not only transport connectivity. Connected but slow: distinguish resource saturation from loss, latency and relay transit. Unexpected peer or stale identity: stop privileged actions and follow the supported revocation/rotation procedure. These are review questions, not commands for an unverified daemon. See data flows and security boundaries. Path policy and advertisement freshness The routing design considers latency, loss, jitter, bandwidth, reliability, headroom, locality and cost only after hard policy constraints. It proposes hysteresis or cooldown behavior to avoid repeatedly switching between similarly ranked paths. Exact thresholds remain implementation decisions, not published service guarantees. Discovery records need a publication/expiry lifecycle and a stale-record response. Cached information can aid recovery but must not perpetuate revoked identity or expired eligibility. Browser-mediated discovery and node participation may have different transport constraints; no unsupported browser networking capability is assumed here."},{"title":"Provider preparation","path":"guides/provider-design.html","text":"Prepare a compute-provider design review Audience: prospective mesh providers. Scope: qualification questions, not installation or a promise of earnings. A compute provider is not automatically an L1 validator; chain node preparation is a different path. Capacity and runtime Inventory the host, available resources, supported runtime/device profile and workload classes. Validate isolation prerequisites for the actual release before offering capacity. Do not infer arbitrary OS or GPU support from a list of architectural technologies. Reachability and identity Plan endpoint identity, key storage/rotation, permitted inbound/outbound paths and relay dependence. Establish how capacity advertisements expire and are revoked. Keep private topology and administrative endpoints out of public listings. Admission and tenancy Define quotas, allowed workload classes, tenant isolation, secret cleanup and warm-runtime reuse. Admission must use current local capacity, not only replicated advertisements. Separate administration/signing credentials from tenant execution. Evidence and operations Record dispatch identity, observed execution state, usage units and retention policy. Establish monitoring, drain, maintenance, incident response and recovery procedures. A signed report proves attribution, not correctness; the agreement must define acceptable evidence and disputes. Commercial obligations Review metering, service acceptance, price units, cancellation, timeout and settlement behavior against released terms/contracts. Include power, hardware, bandwidth, relay and operational costs in your own evaluation. Historical examples are neither revenue projections nor guarantees. Qualification outcome A useful review produces a supported host/runtime profile, a tested workload isolation result, verified network paths, an admission/drain demonstration and a reconciliation test. Until these are available, describe the node as a candidate rather than production-ready capacity. See Mesh lifecycle, connectivity, Marketplace and security."},{"title":"THEO economy","path":"platform/theo-economy.html","text":"The THEO economy THEO is the unit everything on the platform map is priced and settled in: DevHub plans, metered usage, marketplace capacity and (planned) app-ecosystem purchases. This page explains the loop and the settlement mechanics that exist in code. For the display price feed and where THEO trades, see THEO price feed and markets. Nothing here is investment information. The loop Developers pay THEO for DevHub plans and usage (developer usage). Buyers pay THEO for marketplace compute and storage orders. Providers earn THEO from those orders through on-chain settlement, minus the platform fee. Publishers earn THEO when others deploy their apps and agents (planned, see app ecosystem). Validators secure the Autheo L1 that records the settlements (L1 networks). Units Item Value Source Native denomination on the L1 aauth network config Display conversion 1 THEO = 10^18 aauth network config THEO ERC-20 decimals (settlement profiles) 18 DevHub marketplace.rs DevHub catalog amounts hundredths of THEO DevHub checkout_theo_atomic Marketplace and MCP amounts are handled as exact decimal strings, never floating point. Marketplace settlement Source: crates/hive-cloud/src/marketplace.rs and docs/marketplace-settlement-profiles.md. There are two settlement profiles, deliberately separated so test configuration can never repoint production: Profile Network Status base-mainnet-v1 Base mainnet (chain 8453), THEO ERC-20 0xebe516a20238f79dc20b07ead6768e08891ed309 Historical only: existing payments still verify; no new payment intents autheo-testnet-v1 Autheo testnet (chain 785 / 0x311) Available only when an operator sets this exact profile and supplies audited contract addresses The testnet profile is fixed: RPC https://testnet-rpc1.autheo.com, explorer https://testnet-explorer.autheo.com, fee 500 bps, 2 confirmations. Before it creates any intent, DevHub proves the RPC really is chain 785, that the THEO token and settlement contract addresses contain code, and that the token's symbol() is exactly THEO. An explicit audited flag and a configuration reference are also required; contract existence alone never implies approval. One transaction, three recipients Payment goes through MarketplaceAtomicFeeSplit V2, which splits the gross amount atomically and emits: Settlement(bytes32 settlementKey, bytes32 orderReference, address payer, address token, address providerRecipient, uint256 grossAmount, uint256 providerAmount, address feeRecipient, uint256 feeAmount, uint256 feeBps) DevHub derives the expected settlement key from the order (Solidity abi.encode, not packed), computes the event topic at runtime, and only accepts a receipt that matches the snapshotted profile, order, payer, provider and amounts after the confirmation depth. Every payment intent snapshots the complete profile, so later configuration changes cannot reinterpret an old payment. DevHub plan checkout Plan and credit payments are a plain ERC-20 transfer of the exact THEO amount to an operator-configured treasury, verified on chain (developer usage). Which chain that is depends on operator configuration; the code refuses to run checkout if chain, token, treasury, decimals or confirmations are missing or unsafe. Mainnet The Autheo L1 mainnet configuration is published (autheo_2127-1), but no DevHub or Marketplace settlement profile targets it yet. Mainnet settlement is planned. Until then, treat testnet settlement as the integration target and do not send mainnet funds based on these docs. Next L1 testnet and mainnet · THEO price feed and markets"},{"title":"THEO price feed and markets","path":"concepts/theo.html","text":"THEO THEO is the token Autheo uses to price Marketplace resources (compute, storage, bandwidth, hosting). This page covers only what developers can read today; it is not investment information. Display price feed dev.autheo.com publishes a reference price at https://dev.autheo.com/api/market-reference/theo. On 2026-10-12 it returned JSON with these fields: Field Meaning available Whether a price could be fetched price_usd Reference price in USD change_24h_pct 24-hour change, percent liquidity_usd Reported liquidity of the source pool source, source_url Where the price came from (that day: Dexscreener, Base / Hydrex pool) fetched_at_ms Retrieval time (not proof of a recent trade) stale True when the value is too old to use display_only Always true: for display, not for settlement or quotes The Autheo MCP server reads this feed (or an operator-supplied feed) and refuses to convert amounts when the price is stale or unavailable. Marketplace amounts stay exact decimal strings. Where THEO trades autheo-os/theo-atlas (commit 0294acd) is a local read-only market monitor. It covers MEXC THEO/USDT, the Hydrex THEO/USDC pool on Base, and Uniswap v3 THEO/USDG and THEO/NVDA pools on Robinhood Chain. Robinhood Chain pools are not a claim that the Robinhood brokerage lists THEO. It deliberately omits market cap and cross-chain FDV, and treats pool reserves as reported liquidity, not a guaranteed sell quote. Settlement DevHub source contains THEO settlement verification: plan checkout (exact ERC-20 transfer to a treasury) and Marketplace settlement through the MarketplaceAtomicFeeSplit V2 contract on the Autheo testnet (chain 785). DevHub verifies payments; it never sends or signs them. Mainnet settlement is planned. See THEO economy."},{"title":"L1 testnet and mainnet","path":"platform/l1-networks.html","text":"Autheo L1: testnet and mainnet The Autheo L1 is the foundation of the Autheo stack: every layer above it (marketplace, Layer 0 mesh, DevHub, app ecosystem) is priced in THEO and settles here. L0 does not run consensus; it sends payment and settlement facts to the L1 and keeps logs and bulk data off-chain. It is an EVM-capable Cosmos SDK chain (CometBFT consensus, Ethermint EVM; see architecture). It has a testnet, which DevHub's marketplace settlement targets today, and a published mainnet configuration. Testnet (live-probed) Item Value Evidence Network name Autheo Testnet DevHub settlement profile autheo-testnet-v1 EVM chain ID 785 (0x311) DevHub marketplace.rs; confirmed live JSON-RPC https://testnet-rpc1.autheo.com Same; confirmed live Explorer https://testnet-explorer.autheo.com Same; HTTP 200 Currency symbol THEO Same Probe, 2026-10-12 (America/Denver evening): eth_chainId returned 0x311 (785) and eth_blockNumber returned 0x997810 (block 10,057,744). The explorer returned HTTP 200. A plain GET to the RPC returns 405, which is expected for a JSON-RPC endpoint. This shows the endpoint answered; it is not an uptime guarantee. Add testnet to a wallet Use these values in MetaMask, Coinbase Wallet or Keplr (EVM mode): network name Autheo Testnet, RPC URL https://testnet-rpc1.autheo.com, chain ID 785, currency symbol THEO, explorer https://testnet-explorer.autheo.com. Check it yourself: curl -s -X POST https://testnet-rpc1.autheo.com \\ -H 'content-type: application/json' \\ --data '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"eth_chainId\",\"params\":[]}' # expect {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"result\":\"0x311\"} The testnet THEO token and fee-split contract addresses are operator configuration and are not published in these docs. No faucet is documented here. Mainnet (configuration published) Item Value Cosmos chain ID autheo_2127-1 Native denomination aauth (1 THEO = 10^18 aauth) Binary autheod Bech32 prefix autheo Source and full table: network reference. The mainnet EVM chain ID, public RPC and explorer were not verified for this documentation; obtain them from an official, environment-specific source before connecting a wallet. Do not infer an EVM chain ID from the Cosmos chain ID. Which network should I use? You are… Use Building or testing marketplace settlement, THEO payments, contracts Testnet Running a validator or full node The network your operator approved; start with node preparation Reading chain state for an app Testnet for development; mainnet only with a verified endpoint (integration preparation) Next THEO economy · Network reference · API families"},{"title":"Architecture","path":"concepts/architecture.html","text":"Architecture and execution boundaries Implemented chain: source-backed The public application is an EVM-capable Cosmos SDK chain. Its application wiring includes Ethermint EVM and fee-market modules. Its dependency manifest declares Cosmos SDK v0.53.4 and CometBFT v0.38.17 at the reviewed revision. These are source facts, not a claim about the binary running on any endpoint. An older description that the chain is not EVM-compatible is not an appropriate integration guide. General-purpose containers or mesh workloads are also not equivalent to smart-contract execution: EVM execution is part of the chain application, while off-chain workloads have a separate runtime and trust boundary. Separate the planes Plane Responsibility Documentation status Chain Ordered state, transaction execution, application modules Public implementation sources inspected Connectivity Peer identity, discovery, transport, routing Ecosystem design context; AUTHEO runtime not verified here Compute Scheduling and executing general workloads Design context; no supported deployment API established Commercial services Capacity offers, metering, agreements, settlement integration Do not assume a released marketplace contract Developer and agent tools Application lifecycle and delegated operations Product-specific release evidence required What this boundary prevents A chain transaction success does not prove that an off-chain workload ran correctly. A transport connection does not authorize a workload. A configuration file labelled mainnet does not prove current network health. Security and accounting claims must name the component, version, and evidence supporting them. Continue with ecosystem concepts, API families, or the status matrix."},{"title":"Network reference","path":"reference/networks.html","text":"Network configuration reference Checked: 9 October 2026. Not checked: live RPC health, current block height, validator membership, transaction execution, or endpoint ownership. The public network repository identifies itself as the official configuration source. Its inspected tree contains mainnet artifacts. A README reference to testnet in the chain repo does not supply a verified testnet configuration in this snapshot. Item Published value or artifact Evidence Cosmos chain ID in mainnet configuration autheo_2127-1 chain-id Native denomination aauth network README Published display conversion 1 THEO = 10^18 aauth network README Binary name autheod network README Bech32 prefix autheo network README Genesis mainnet/genesis.json source file Peer and seed configuration mainnet/persistent_peers.txt, mainnet/seeds.txt configuration tree These are published configuration values, not financial guidance or a guarantee that a particular service is available. Do not infer a JSON-RPC numeric chain ID, explorer URL, faucet URL, or bridge address from the table. Obtain those from a reviewed environment-specific source before connecting a wallet. Continue with node preparation or integration preparation."},{"title":"Integration preparation","path":"guides/integration.html","text":"Prepare a chain integration Scope: read-only preparation. This is not a tested mainnet transaction tutorial. Gather the contract first Select the supported release and record its source revision. Obtain an endpoint from an authorized operator and identify whether it serves Cosmos REST, gRPC, CometBFT RPC, or Ethereum JSON-RPC. They are different interfaces. Confirm network identity using that interface's supported method and compare it with the approved network configuration. Confirm authentication, request limits, CORS policy, supported methods, and retention requirements with the operator. Start with a read-only request. Do not sign, broadcast, bridge, or approve token spending during connectivity debugging. A source-defined REST query The reviewed protobuf declares a GET mapping for the AUTHEO module parameters: # Set this only to a Cosmos REST base URL confirmed by your operator. export AUTHEO_REST_URL='https://your-approved-rest-host.example' curl --fail-with-body --silent --show-error \\ \"${AUTHEO_REST_URL%/}/autheo/v1/params\" Replace the example host. This command is illustrative and was not run against a live AUTHEO endpoint. A 404 can mean the wrong API family, a disabled gateway, or a release mismatch—not an absent chain. The API reference identifies the source mapping. Before transaction support Add an explicit network check, fee estimation, account sequence handling, signing isolation, user confirmation, broadcast response handling, and final outcome verification based on the selected release. Do not assume a UI acknowledgement is settlement. Test failures, retries, duplicate submissions, and cancellation in an approved non-production environment. Use troubleshooting to report failures safely."},{"title":"Node preparation","path":"guides/node-operator.html","text":"Prepare to operate a node This guide is a preflight, not a production install recipe. No node was installed or joined during this review. Select matching software and configuration The chain repository provides operator instructions. The official network repository holds configuration artifacts. Pin a release and a matching configuration revision before downloading or executing anything. Do not use an unqualified latest image as your reproducibility record. The reviewed source go.mod declares Go 1.25.8; its README still mentions Go 1.22 and Cosmos SDK v0.47. Resolve build requirements against the chosen release's manifest and CI rather than copying those stale README values. Full node versus validator A full node operation and validator admission are different tasks. The upstream README describes a Sovereign license-binding prerequisite before validator creation. Verify the current release's complete admission flow with maintainers; this docs site does not grant validator eligibility or describe a bypass. Operator checklist Verify release provenance and checksums through the upstream distribution process. Match chain ID and genesis to the approved network configuration. Allocate storage, networking, and host resources from release-specific requirements; no unverified sizing numbers are supplied here. Separate public API access from signing and administrative access. Establish metrics, backup/restore testing, upgrade rehearsal, and a responsible operator. Document rollback limits before upgrading; restoring files is not automatically a safe chain rollback. Validate health without exposing signing keys or private infrastructure details. If prerequisites are unclear, stop before validator creation and ask the release owner. See network reference and security boundaries."},{"title":"Ecosystem concepts","path":"concepts/ecosystem.html","text":"The AUTHEO ecosystem AUTHEO's design connects application development, independently supplied compute, commercial coordination, and a settlement chain. The intended experience is to describe a workload once, choose acceptable capacity and terms, run it on the mesh, and observe both execution and accounting without confusing the two. Status: this page explains design intent, not general availability. The chain has separately reviewed public implementation evidence; the other components below have planning evidence but no release-tested workflow in this review. See capability status. Components and their jobs Component Intended responsibility Does not replace Connectivity Identify peers, discover addresses, establish sessions, repair routes Workload authorization or proof of trustworthy execution L0 Mesh Admit and execute workloads on provider nodes; report health and results Chain consensus or commercial agreement policy Marketplace Describe offers, match demand, coordinate usage evidence and settlement The execution runtime or the buyer's signing decision DevHub and OpenEdge Turn source and deployment intent into observable project/build/deployment workflows Provider capacity, marketplace rules or a transaction ledger Freddie Carry out delegated, persistent operational workflows User authorization, spending limits or settlement verification AUTHEO Chain / L1 Execute chain transactions and maintain ordered application state General-purpose hosting and workload correctness Here AUTHEO means the whole ecosystem. Use connectivity layer for the peer-network substrate, not AUTHEO alone. L0 is the compute-layer name in this design; it is not a claim that all application traffic is blockchain traffic. Agentic OS groups the developer interfaces and Freddie; it is not a separate kernel or consensus network. Who uses what? Application developers use DevHub to express builds, resources, environments and deployments. Start with the application lifecycle. Compute buyers compare raw resources or packaged services and acceptance terms. Start with Marketplace. Providers advertise eligible capacity, run workloads and produce accountable usage records. Start with provider preparation. Chain integrators and validators use versioned chain interfaces and network configuration, not a mesh installation recipe. Start with integration or node preparation. Security reviewers examine permissions, provider trust, runtime isolation and signing independently. Start with security boundaries. One workload, three different flows Intent and control: source revision → GitOps synchronization → DevHub deployment intent → Marketplace admission and placement → node admission. Execution and data: artifact/input transfer → isolated runtime → service traffic or result → logs and completion evidence. Agreement and accounting: buyer-approved terms → supported chain agreement mechanism → attributable usage/completion evidence → settlement outcome → reconciliation. These are logical design relationships, not a released API sequence. Logs and large artifacts belong off-chain in the design; durable settlement facts may be recorded on-chain. A successful build is not a running workload, and a running workload is not proof of settled payment. Data flows details the handoffs. What the design makes possible The planning covers container services, functions, batch work, builds, AI/GPU jobs, edge APIs, storage and packaged services such as game-server hosting. It also describes developer services that combine application support with infrastructure. These are intended workload classes, not a tested support matrix, available catalogue, pricing promise or service-level agreement. For a specific use case, first state the runtime, data sensitivity, recovery requirements, region and budget. Then establish which released component can satisfy each requirement. Do not substitute an architectural aspiration for release evidence."},{"title":"Data flows and handoffs","path":"concepts/data-flows.html","text":"Architecture handoffs and data flows Design / planned, except for the separately source-backed chain references. This page makes ownership and failure boundaries explicit; it does not specify released endpoints or message schemas. Deployment and execution Source revision → GitOps synchronization → DevHub intent ↓ Marketplace eligibility and matching ↓ Provider/node admission ↓ Artifact and inputs → Mesh runtime → Service traffic / results ↓ Logs, usage and outcome evidence The build artifact must be attributable to the selected revision. The accepted workload must be attributable to its request and provider. Logs are useful for operations but may contain sensitive inputs; they are not automatically public or suitable for permanent chain storage. Commercial and chain state Buyer reviews terms → Authorized agreement action ↓ Supported chain mechanism ↑ Dispatch + usage + acceptance evidence → Reconciliation ↓ Verified settlement state / unresolved dispute The exact agreement mechanism is unresolved in this review. “Settlement pending” must remain separate from “execution failed.” A timeout observed by a client does not prove that an action had no effect. Before retrying, determine whether the original request was accepted or settled. What belongs where? Data Intended authority/location Important limit Source and build inputs Developer-controlled source/build system A branch name alone is not immutable provenance Deployment intent Developer control plane Desired state is not observed runtime state Capacity and health Provider advertisements and mesh observations Freshness and reservation are required Workload secrets and application data Approved runtime/storage boundary Never infer confidentiality from encrypted transport alone Usage and completion evidence Attributable records correlated with dispatch Signature attribution is not independent correctness proof Payment outcome Chosen chain/contract state Confirm against the selected release and network Reputation Evidence-derived provider assessment Scoring and appeals need a defined policy Identity boundaries A repository identity, developer account, mesh endpoint identity, provider identity, agent identity and wallet/validator signer serve different purposes. The design needs explicit bindings and revocation rules between them. Reusing a key or equating two identifiers without a contract obscures authority and makes incident recovery harder. Failure ownership DevHub should identify invalid configuration or build failure. Marketplace should explain admission, terms and reconciliation failures. Mesh should distinguish no-start, running, failed and unknown execution outcomes. Chain integration should distinguish submission from verified transaction outcome. Freddie should preserve the caller's authority and report the state of the whole task across those systems. See ecosystem roles, Marketplace and security."},{"title":"Security boundaries","path":"concepts/security.html","text":"Security boundaries No blanket security claims This documentation is not an audit certificate. It does not establish that all AUTHEO traffic uses post-quantum cryptography, that a workload is isolated on every host, that keys never leave hardware, or that the entire ecosystem is non-custodial. Treat each claim as a versioned assertion that needs implementation evidence and a deployment configuration. A library's capabilities do not prove AUTHEO has integrated or enabled them. Protect the right assets Wallet and validator keys: separate signing authority from ordinary application access; follow the supported signer procedure for your chosen release. API credentials: use the endpoint operator's documented scopes and storage requirements. Never put credentials in browser-delivered documentation or examples. Node data: test restore and upgrade procedures in an isolated environment before production changes. Agent actions: require clear authorization boundaries and a way to revoke delegated access. Workload secrets: do not assume an execution provider is trusted merely because transport is encrypted. When something goes wrong Stop the affected operation, retain non-sensitive logs, record versions and times, and contact the operator through an independently verified channel. Do not paste secret material into public issues. This review did not verify a dedicated AUTHEO vulnerability-reporting address, so none is invented here. Sources and troubleshooting describe the scope of verification. Design threat boundaries Boundary Intended control to evaluate What it does not establish Peer session Identity binding, authenticated transport, authorization An honest provider or permission to run any workload Tenant runtime Supported isolation profile, quotas and cleanup Immunity to host compromise or device-side exposure Workload supply chain Artifact provenance and controlled deployment inputs Correctness of arbitrary application code Provider evidence Attributable dispatch, usage and outcome records Independent proof that every reported result is true Agent delegation Explicit action scope, caps, confirmation and revocation Unlimited authority inherited from a connected account Payment Verified network, contract and signer authority Legal classification or automatic refunds from prose Reconcile security vocabulary The design discusses microVM isolation, software sandboxes and hardware-assisted confidential execution. They protect different boundaries; naming several technologies does not prove a composed security property. Specify the host/runtime/device profile and test the actual tenant boundary. Warm-pool reuse also needs cleanup and tenant-state rules. Post-quantum key exchange and post-quantum signatures are separate requirements. A transport proposal does not establish protection for wallet signing, software updates, stored data or every fallback path. This review does not claim an enabled algorithm suite. For sensitive workloads, require a documented threat model, supported versions, deployment configuration, key lifecycle, revocation behavior, independent review where appropriate and reproducible failure tests. Reject absolute claims such as “a tenant cannot affect another tenant” without a scoped assurance case. See Freddie permission boundaries, Mesh runtime choices, and data ownership."},{"title":"API reference","path":"reference/api.html","text":"Source-backed API reference Evidence level: source-defined, not live endpoint-tested. Reviewed source: proto/autheo/query.proto. This is a bounded reference to one module, not the entire AUTHEO API. Interface families Cosmos REST gateway: HTTP mappings declared in protobuf annotations. gRPC: protobuf service and message contracts. CometBFT RPC: consensus/node interface, not an interchangeable REST base URL. Ethereum JSON-RPC: EVM-facing interface; enabled methods and server configuration must be checked for your release. AUTHEO module queries gRPC query Declared HTTP mapping Request data ContractByDenom GET /autheo/v1/contract_by_denom denom query field DenomByContract GET /autheo/v1/denom_by_contract/{contract} contract path field Params GET /autheo/v1/params Empty request Permissions GET /autheo/v1/permissions address query field BlockList GET /autheo/v1/blocklist Empty request ReplayBlock No HTTP annotation in reviewed file Protobuf request with Ethereum messages and block metadata ContractByDenomResponse declares contract and auto_contract strings. DenomByContractResponse declares a denom string. QueryParamsResponse wraps a Params message. QueryPermissionsResponse declares can_change_token_mapping and can_turn_bridge booleans. QueryBlockListResponse contains a blob bytes field. Follow the referenced schema for serialization and types; do not infer runtime permissions from a query name. Full schema navigation Protobuf tree Generated Swagger artifact Application wiring A generated specification can lag deployed code. For an integration contract, compare the chosen release, generated API artifacts, server configuration, and observed responses. Authentication, quotas, error envelopes, availability, and public endpoint addresses were not established by this review. See integration preparation for a deliberately read-only example."},{"title":"Status and support boundaries","path":"reference/status.html","text":"Status and support boundaries Status here means what this documentation review established, not the product's operational status page. Capability Evidence What remains unknown Cosmos SDK / CometBFT chain Pinned dependency manifest and application source Software actually running on each network endpoint EVM integration Ethermint EVM imports, keeper and module wiring Enabled endpoint methods and release compatibility Mainnet configuration Official configuration artifacts Current liveness and peer reachability AUTHEO module query contracts Protobuf source mappings Live gateway exposure, auth, quotas, response behavior Connectivity / L0 Mesh Detailed peer, execution and node-lifecycle design Release, supported runtime profiles, tested discovery/admission/recovery DevHub / OpenEdge Project, build and GitOps handoff design Released portal/integration, auth, schema, rollback workflow Marketplace Offering, matching, evidence and settlement-intent design Contract state/authority, metering/disputes, released terms Freddie Persistent workflow and delegation design Runtime release, enforceable scopes/caps/revocation/recovery Quantum-resilient transport No AUTHEO deployment proof established in this review Actual enabled algorithms, negotiation and key lifecycle Security, performance, SLA and compliance No certification established by this review Applicable independent assurance and approved claims Status vocabulary Source-backed: an identified revision contains the implementation or declaration. Configuration-published: an owned repository publishes a network artifact. Live-tested: a dated test against a named environment established a specific behavior. None is claimed in this update. Design / planned: intended behavior without reviewed release evidence. Unknown: required evidence has not been established; this does not mean the feature is absent. Move a feature to supported documentation Provide the owner, public source or approved artifact, exact version, supported environment, successful reproducible test, known failure modes, and publication approval. Follow contribution rules. Record a new verification date rather than silently replacing evidence."},{"title":"What is live (dated probes)","path":"reference/live-status.html","text":"What is live (dated probes) This page records what anonymous HTTP requests returned on 2026-10-12 (about 18:20 America/Denver). It is a snapshot, not a monitoring service. For which capabilities are backed by source code, see the status matrix. dev.autheo.com (Autheo DevHub) URL Result Meaning /, /docs, /docs/getting-started, /docs/api-reference 200 Public site and docs are served /pricing, /status, /blog 200 Public pages are served /llms.txt 200 Served; still describes the product under its earlier working name /api/market-reference/theo 200, JSON THEO display price feed (see THEO) /projects, /deployments, /run-node 307 → /sign-in Dashboard; needs an account /marketplace 307 → /integrations Marketplace entry point moved under integrations API hosts Host Result https://api.shadw.cloud/healthz 200 (ok): the DevHub API host the dashboard's API reference currently uses (legacy name) https://api.autheo.dev No connection (does not resolve), although the live getting-started page names it https://autheo.dev No connection, although page metadata names it as the organisation URL Autheo L1 testnet (probed about 18:45) Endpoint Result https://testnet-rpc1.autheo.com eth_chainId 0x311 (chain 785) https://testnet-rpc1.autheo.com eth_blockNumber 0x997810 (block 10,057,744) https://testnet-explorer.autheo.com 200 https://marketplace.autheo.dev No connection (the production Marketplace URL DevHub defaults to) See L1 testnet and mainnet. Related sites Site Result autheo.com 308 → www.autheo.com autheo-stack.shadw.app 200: architecture narrative autheo-mcp-site.vercel.app 200: MCP marketing site, still shows v0.2 (39 tools); the generated reference is current This documentation 200 (GitHub Pages) Not checked Mainnet RPC/REST endpoints, validator liveness and anything behind sign-in were not tested. The Autheo MCP has been verified against local fixtures only."},{"title":"Sources and verification","path":"reference/sources.html","text":"Sources and verification Review date: 9 October 2026. Public source inspection was read-only. No wallet credentials, private planning exports, or live transaction payloads were sent to these sources. Primary evidence ID Source What it supports C1 Chain go.mod Declared Go, Cosmos SDK, CometBFT and Ethermint dependencies C2 Application wiring EVM integration in source C3 Query protobuf Bounded API mappings and message fields C4 Chain README Repository ownership/context; operator links, with version drift noted N1 Network README Published denomination, prefix, binary and configuration role N2 Mainnet chain ID Exact published Cosmos chain ID Chain revision: 976ec4328d342a07e6ef7eb9ecaec222c55f29e1. Network revision: a02ac6576dd3b687effc0d741a71a88b094aba21. Evidence precedence For implementation details, prefer the selected release's code, manifests, schemas, and tests over an unversioned narrative. The reviewed chain README still names older Go and SDK versions than its manifest. A source implementation also does not prove that every network enables it. Limits This review did not deploy the chain, run an AUTHEO SDK, verify mainnet transactions, attest infrastructure, audit cryptography, or obtain legal conclusions. Broader ecosystem implementation remains unverified; the component guides explain design intent separately. Status makes those limits visible by component. Design evidence versus implementation evidence The component and lifecycle guides are editorial consolidations of ecosystem planning reviewed on the date above. They preserve component responsibilities, workload classes and intended handoffs while removing internal organization/legal material, example prices, unsupported deployment claims and speculative commands. Their Design / planned labels apply to the entire described workflow. Planning evidence supports an explanation of intent, not a claim that a service is operating. Public chain evidence above takes precedence for chain implementation details. A private source-to-page review ledger is retained for maintainers; private documents and paths are not exported by this site. When a release is verified, add its public source, version, environment and test result rather than erasing the design/implementation distinction."},{"title":"Glossary","path":"reference/glossary.html","text":"Glossary Term Meaning in these docs AUTHEO Chain / L1 Chain application and its consensus-backed state; source includes EVM integration L0 Mesh Design vocabulary for off-chain coordination and compute execution Cosmos chain ID String identifying a Cosmos network configuration; not interchangeable with a JSON-RPC chain ID EVM Ethereum Virtual Machine execution environment; distinct from a general-purpose workload host REST gateway HTTP interface mapped to protobuf service methods gRPC Typed service interface defined by protobuf Genesis Initial chain state/configuration artifact that must match the intended network THEO / aauth Display unit and native denomination as published by the network source; see network reference DevHub Developer-facing project and deployment concept Marketplace Commercial coordination concept; not proof of a released payment contract Freddie Agent-assisted operations concept; delegated permissions require a separate contract OpenEdge GitOps Planned bridge from versioned application source to DevHub desired deployment state Agentic OS Umbrella name for developer interfaces and Freddie, not a separate operating-system kernel Control plane Intent, admission, scheduling, authorization and policy decisions Data plane Artifact/input movement, workload execution, service traffic and results Fluid Compute Proposed warm-runtime reuse; no guaranteed start time Peer / endpoint identity Identity used to address and authenticate a mesh participant; distinct from wallet authority CRDT Mergeable replicated-state model proposed for operational synchronization; not payment consensus Attestation Evidence about a specified platform/property, not universal proof of safe behavior DaaS Developer-as-a-Service: proposed application/support offerings, potentially bundled with hosting Drain Stop admitting new work while completing or safely recovering existing work Admission Decision that current policy and capacity permit a particular workload Settlement Verified accounting/payment outcome under a specified chain mechanism Source-backed Supported by a particular source revision, without claiming live deployment Platform map The three Autheo surfaces (DevHub, app ecosystem, compute/storage marketplace) inside the THEO economy on the L1; see platform map Autheo Labs The team behind DevHub, the first go-to-market surface Autheo testnet EVM chain ID 785 (0x311), RPC testnet-rpc1.autheo.com; target of the marketplace settlement profile Placement policy Marketplace order data that pins which approved nodes may run a deployment; DevHub never falls back to other nodes Marketplace release Immutable, publishable version of a DevHub project with declared capabilities; the unit of app distribution AtomicFeeSplit V2 Settlement contract that pays provider and fee recipient in one THEO transaction and emits a Settlement event Workflow run One execution of a durable DevHub workflow; steps, events and hooks are persisted and can be replayed Browser node A browser tab donated as a mesh node via /run-node; its requests are counted but not billed today Active CPU Billing unit that charges only non-idle CPU time See architecture, networks, and status."},{"title":"FAQ","path":"reference/faq.html","text":"FAQ What is Autheo? A Cosmos SDK / CometBFT blockchain with EVM support, plus a developer cloud (DevHub) that runs workloads on a peer-to-peer compute fabric, a resource Marketplace priced in THEO, agent tooling (Autheo MCP) and an agent trust layer (Identity and agent trust). The ecosystem overview shows how they relate. Is dev.autheo.com live? Partly. On 2026-10-12 the public pages (home, docs, pricing, status, blog) were served and the dashboard required sign-in. Details: DevHub → What is live today. What is the API base URL? Everything starts at dev.autheo.com: sign in there and create an API key. The API is currently served from https://api.shadw.cloud, a legacy hostname that answered /healthz with ok on 2026-10-12 (the live getting-started page names https://api.autheo.dev, which did not resolve that day). A self-hosted node serves the API on http://127.0.0.1:8786. Why do I sometimes see \"shadw\"? It is the earlier working name of Autheo DevHub. A few code identifiers still use it: the CLI binary shadw, its config in ~/.shadw/, SHADW_* environment variables and the legacy API host api.shadw.cloud. The product is Autheo DevHub at dev.autheo.com; the node software is called Hive. How do I deploy something? Sign in at dev.autheo.com, create an API key, then use shadw deploy or POST /v1/git/deploy. See DevHub. Can I host a game server? Yes, DevHub has game server presets (for example Team Fortress 2, Don't Starve Together, GoldenEye: Source) with a guided setup in the dashboard. Can my AI agent use Autheo? Yes, read-only, through the Autheo MCP server: 44 tools covering Marketplace, DevHub, chain reads, THEO display price and agent-trust inspection. It sends no transactions and was verified against local fixtures, not a live installation. Can an agent spend money or sign transactions? No. Neither the MCP server nor the agent trust prototype holds wallet keys, makes payments or executes transactions. Agent trust budgets are simulated DEMO units. Do I need to register an identity for my agent? No. Agent identities are did:key DIDs you create offline. Authority comes from a signed delegation chain back to a root DID the verifier trusts. See Identity and agent trust. Where is THEO priced? DevHub/Marketplace expose a THEO reference price (/api/market-reference/theo on dev.autheo.com returned JSON on 2026-10-12). The MCP tools refuse to convert with stale or unavailable prices. Is the code open source? It varies: Autheo MCP and the Agent Environment are MIT. DevHub's LICENSE.md is restrictive and is not MIT. Check each repository. Something here is wrong or out of date. Every page lists its sources in provenance.json. Open an issue or pull request on autheo-os/autheo-dev-docs; see Contributing."},{"title":"Documentation changelog","path":"reference/changelog.html","text":"Documentation changelog Changes to this documentation site. Product release notes live in each product repository. 2026-10-12 (layered stack) Restructured around the layered stack: the Autheo L1 underlies everything; the marketplace sells Layer 0 capacity; DevHub deploys to the L0 mesh; the app ecosystem sits on top. New diagram, new pages The Autheo stack (layers plus one deployment from push to settlement) and Layer 0 mesh (network, compute, data, identity fabrics from L0_devhub_deploy 29abc9f). 2026-10-12 (platform map) New: Platform section organised around the go-to-market vision: platform map with diagram, Build with DevHub (IDE toolkit, full-stack SDKs, workflow/DAG engine, agentic OS), compute and storage marketplace, developer usage and billing, app ecosystem, THEO economy, L1 testnet and mainnet, developer journeys and a roadmap scorecard. Evidence: L0_devhub_deploy 29abc9f, dev.autheo.com and testnet RPC probes. Live probe: Autheo testnet RPC answered eth_chainId = 0x311 (785) at block 10,057,744; explorer HTTP 200. Navigation regrouped by surface: Build, Run, Ship, Settle. Home, getting started and glossary link into the platform section. 2026-10-12 Naming: DevHub pages use Autheo DevHub / dev.autheo.com throughout and cite autheo-os/L0_devhub_deploy (now synced to 29abc9f); shadw appears only where code identifiers still use it. New: MCP server section. Install, configuration and client setup for Autheo MCP, plus a tool reference generated from autheo-MCP 6634165 (44 tools, 5 resources). New: DevHub (Layer 0) section. Overview with dated live-status probes of dev.autheo.com, authentication, CLI and API deploy flow, and an API and CLI reference generated from L0_devhub_deploy 29abc9f (34 endpoints, 14 shadw commands). New concept pages: Identity and agent trust (DIDs, delegation chains, receipts, local trust service) and The Autheo agent operating stack. New: FAQ and this changelog. Build: page counts come from publication.json; provenance.json records sources for added pages; CI verifies the build, internal links, provenance, and that both generated references match their data. 2026-10-09 Initial 23-page review published as plain HTML with local search."},{"title":"Troubleshooting","path":"troubleshooting/index.html","text":"Troubleshooting A chain request returns 404 or HTML Confirm the endpoint's API family. An explorer website, EVM JSON-RPC endpoint, and Cosmos REST gateway are not interchangeable. Confirm the release exposes the source-defined route. Do not retry a state-changing request blindly. Chain identity does not match Stop before signing. Compare the environment with trusted network configuration. Do not repair a mismatch by changing a wallet setting to a guessed value. Instructions mention a missing CLI or product Check status. Design documents are not installable packages. Ask for a release artifact rather than trying a similarly named package from an unrelated publisher. Docs build fails Use the locked dependencies and the documented Node version. Run npm ci --ignore-scripts, then npm run check. A missing allowlisted page, unsafe source path, symlink, or broken local link must fail the build; do not suppress dead-link errors to ship it. Navigation or assets fail under a subpath Rebuild with DOCS_BASE set to the exact leading-and-trailing-slash mount path. Publish the contents of dist only. Do not serve the repository root and do not use a single-page HTML fallback for missing Markdown or JavaScript files. Report a problem safely Include the page URL, expected behavior, non-sensitive error text, browser or software version, network name, and time observed. Exclude keys, seed phrases, API tokens, personal data, and internal hostnames. Contributors should use the repository's private review channel until a public support destination is approved."},{"title":"Contributing","path":"contributing/index.html","text":"Contributing to AUTHEO documentation Publication is explicit Only pages listed in publication.json are staged into the site build. Legacy source material, private planning, research, compliance drafts, and audit reports are not public site inputs. A file being tracked by Git does not authorize its publication. Author a page Choose a reader task and scope. Add one clear title, prerequisites, steps or definitions, expected outcomes, failure cases, and related pages. Support product claims with a pinned public source or approved evidence. Distinguish source-backed, live-tested, planned, and unknown. Use placeholder hosts for examples unless an endpoint is owned, current, and reviewed. Never include credentials or executable install scripts copied without review. Add the page to the allowlist and sidebar together; no orphan public pages. Run the tests, inventory, production build, local link checks, and desktop/mobile review. Open a reviewed branch change. Do not publish directly from the planning repository root. Review sensitive claims Network identifiers, token units, payment behavior, security algorithms, benchmark numbers, uptime promises, legal status, and compliance statements require specific evidence and owner review. Remove an unsupported public claim rather than making it sound more confident. Preserve history Keep source material recoverable. The private migration inventory records legacy disposition. Redirect only when the new page actually answers the same question; otherwise archive explicitly. Do not expose private source paths through a public redirect map. See deployment and sources."},{"title":"Standalone deployment","path":"deployment/index.html","text":"Serve these docs as static files This repository contains the approved public documentation for autheo-os/autheo-dev-docs. All 23 documents are committed as ordinary HTML. Serve the repository root: no build, framework, backend, CDN or SPA fallback is required. Reading and navigation work without JavaScript; local search is an optional enhancement. Read the live documentation. GitHub Pages publishes the committed files from main / repository root, using the existing .nojekyll file and HTTPS. No project build command or custom deployment workflow is required. The GitHub repository is the source view, not the rendered website. This documentation deployment does not verify an AUTHEO product deployment. The site retains noindex, nofollow pending a separate indexing decision; this is not access control. Simplest host settings Publish directory: repository root (./). Build command: none. Entry file: index.html, already committed. Serve normal .html, .css, .js and .svg files with their normal content types. Do not enable a catch-all SPA rewrite. Each document has a real file, including directory index.html files. The same files work at / and /autheo-dev-docs/, or another subpath. Links and assets are relative; there is no mount-specific compilation. For example, reference/api.html is an ordinary file and remains readable when opened directly or reloaded. Preview without installing dependencies With Node.js 22 or newer, run from this repository root: node scripts/serve.mjs Open the printed loopback URL. Stop with Ctrl+C. This small preview server has no rewrites and serves only the static-file allowlist, excluding source files, maintenance scripts, Git history, dependencies and test artifacts. No package installation is required to preview the committed viewer. Any ordinary static file server can also display it. When publishing a tracked repository root, maintenance files and the approved Markdown sources may also be reachable; they are public source, not secrets. Never upload an unfiltered local working directory containing ignored artifacts or credentials. Deny dotfiles, especially .git, and exclude node_modules, .gm and artifacts. For a static-only upload, use the explicit generated page/asset allowlist or the optional dist copy below. Edit and regenerate The Markdown sources remain under site. publication.json is the exact 23-page public allowlist. Use the pinned build-time parser only when updating generated HTML: npm ci --ignore-scripts npm run docs:build npm run check First regenerate the root HTML and local search index with docs:build. The check then runs source safety, provenance and generated-output parity tests, regenerates again and checks all local links, assets and fragments at both mounts. If adapting the deployment page, update its publishedSha256 in provenance.json before checking; do not change the other 22 source-page hashes. Commit the generated files together with source changes. No runtime dependency is needed by the resulting viewer. The same command also creates dist/ containing only allowlisted HTML and assets. If an existing host already publishes dist, keep that publish directory and use this build command: npm ci --ignore-scripts && npm run docs:build This is optional compatibility for existing build-based hosting. The default remains root, no build. The docs:build:pages command is an alias for the same portable output, not a different build. Verify before release npm run test:browser npm run export Browser tests start and stop their own loopback ordinary-file server. They cover every direct HTML URL and reload at root and project subpath, desktop and mobile viewports, navigation, keyboard access, local search, no-JavaScript reading and WCAG-tagged automated rules. Set CHROMIUM_PATH to an installed Chromium executable if necessary. Automated checks are not full accessibility certification. Reports and screenshots stay ignored under artifacts. The export command writes an independent allowlisted source package, including committed static output, to artifacts/standalone-docs. It excludes private material, ignored artifacts, dependencies and Git history. It is not automatically pushed or uploaded. provenance.json retains original source hashes, with only this deployment page adapted to the plain-file viewer. The inactive ci/verify.yml.example template checks the same portable artifact. It remains outside .github/workflows and does not deploy. No credential escalation is needed to use the viewer. Hosting status and 404 diagnosis On 9 October 2026 (America/Denver), the repository source was accessible, but GitHub Pages was not configured and the documentation website returned HTTP 404. Committing index.html alone had not activated hosting. GitHub Pages was then enabled for main / repository root, and the initial deployment completed successfully. The public homepage and API reference returned HTTP 200 and byte-matched the committed HTML. To verify an update, check the repository Actions page for the successful pages-build-deployment run at the intended commit, then open the homepage and a direct document such as API reference. The Pages source must remain main / (root). A GitHub blob URL is a source-code view, not the documentation viewer. If a previously copied link returns 404, use the live documentation link above and its navigation. Document URLs end in .html; Markdown source paths and unconfigured extensionless routes are not the published document URLs. No catch-all rewrite masks missing pages. Verify both HTTP status and expected page content, including a direct reload, before claiming an update is live. Operator release checklist Preserve source-backed, design/planned and unknown status statements. Keep all 23 reviewed public documents and confidentiality exclusions. Confirm applicable rights and official support/security contacts; this viewer adds neither a license grant nor invented contacts. If regenerating, install from the lockfile and review dependency advisories. Verify direct links, refresh, search, keyboard access and mobile navigation at the actual host URL. Decide separately whether to remove noindex or configure a domain. Publish only approved files; retain the previous Git revision for rollback. Smoke-test the actual public URL and record the release commit and date. Local checks alone do not establish live deployment."}];
